        // Section 1: Compare and contrast various techniques and considerations f (24 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques_",
            title: "Compare and contrast various techniques and considerations for designing a data model for the Customer 360 Platform",
            color: Color(red: 0.30, green: 0.50, blue: 0.80),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.94, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "doc.text.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__obj",
                    title: "Compare and contrast various techniques and considerations for designing a data model for the Customer 360 Platform",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__1", number: 1, question: "When designing a data model for a multi-cloud Salesforce org, what are the key considerations for choosing between lookup relationships and master-detail relationships?", answer: "Master-detail (MD) creates a tight parent-child bond: the child cannot exist without the parent, OWD of the child follows the parent, and roll-up summary fields are enabled. Lookups are looser: the child can exist independently, security is separate, and no roll-ups are available natively. In a multi-cloud org, use MD when lifecycle and sharing must be tied together (e.g., Case-to-Account in Service Cloud). Use lookup when cross-cloud objects have independent lifecycles or when the target object already has two MD parents (the platform limit). Cascade delete on MD is automatic; with lookup it is optional and configurable."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__2", number: 2, question: "What are the trade-offs between denormalized and normalized data models in Salesforce, particularly in relation to SOQL query complexity and governor limits?", answer: "Normalized models reduce data redundancy but require more SOQL joins via relationship queries, increasing query complexity and the risk of hitting the 100-query governor limit per transaction. Denormalized models duplicate data across objects for faster, simpler queries and fewer joins, but increase storage costs and risk of data inconsistency. In Salesforce, where cross-object formula fields and roll-ups are limited, a moderate denormalization strategy (e.g., storing a key parent value on the child record) is common to avoid expensive relationship traversals in triggers and batch jobs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__3", number: 3, question: "What is the maximum number of master-detail relationships per object and how does it constrain data model design for complex domain models?", answer: "Each custom object can have a maximum of 2 master-detail relationships. This hard limit means an object can have at most two controlling parents. For complex domain models requiring 3+ controlling parents, architects must fall back to lookup relationships (losing roll-up summaries and implicit sharing) or redesign using junction objects. This constraint forces deliberate choices about which parent relationship most needs roll-up, cascade delete, and sharing inheritance."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__4", number: 4, question: "What is the maximum number of lookup relationships per object and how does it constrain data model design for complex domain models?", answer: "Custom objects can have up to 40 relationship fields per object (including lookups and master-detail). While this is generous for most designs, each lookup adds a field, consumes from the custom field limit, and can complicate SOQL joins and UI page layouts. In complex domain models, excessive lookups signal a need to reconsider normalization or to introduce junction objects. More critically, SOQL only supports up to 5 levels of relationship traversal, so deeply nested lookups still limit query capability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__5", number: 5, question: "What are the limits and behaviors of roll-up summary fields in Salesforce?", answer: "Roll-up summary fields (COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX) are only available on the parent of a master-detail relationship. Key limits: up to 40 roll-up summary fields per object, cannot reference fields from related objects (only the immediate child), and cannot use certain field types like formula fields in SUM/MIN/MAX. They trigger recalculation synchronously on child record changes, which can cause locking on the parent in high-volume scenarios. Cross-object formulas and roll-ups cannot be chained (a roll-up field cannot itself be rolled up again at a grandparent level in most scenarios)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__6", number: 6, question: "How do roll-up summary fields influence decisions about parent-child object relationships in a high-volume environment?", answer: "In high-volume environments, every child record save triggers synchronous recalculation of the parent's roll-up summary fields, causing a lock on the parent record. With thousands of concurrent child record DML operations on the same parent, this creates lock contention and \"unable to lock row\" errors. Architects mitigate this by: switching to lookup relationships and computing aggregates asynchronously (batch Apex or scheduled jobs), using async processing, or partitioning data to reduce children per parent (e.g., sub-accounts). The 40 roll-up field limit also constrains how many metrics can be maintained at the parent level."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__7", number: 7, question: "How does the choice between formula fields and custom metadata types affect data model maintainability at scale?", answer: "Formula fields embed logic directly in the object metadata, making them easy to read but hard to update at scale—changing a widely used formula requires a metadata deployment. Custom Metadata Types (CMT) store configuration as deployable, versionable records, allowing logic changes without formula edits. CMTs are preferred for threshold values, feature flags, or business rules that change frequently or differ by environment (sandbox vs. production), because they can be included in change sets and packages while remaining editable by admins post-deployment."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__8", number: 8, question: "How does the choice between formula fields and custom metadata types affect governor limits at scale?", answer: "Formula fields are computed at query time and do not consume DML or SOQL governor limits, but complex formulas on LDV objects can slow query performance because they cannot be indexed. Custom Metadata Types are queried via SOQL and cached; CMT queries do not count against the standard SOQL limit of 100 queries per transaction (they have a separate limit). However, accessing CMT records in bulk Apex is more efficient via the static map pattern (load once, use repeatedly) to avoid repeated SOQL calls inside loops."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__9", number: 9, question: "What are the implications of using a junction object versus a many-to-many relationship with multiple lookup fields for cascade delete behavior?", answer: "A junction object with two master-detail relationships gets cascade delete from both parents: deleting either parent deletes all junction records. This can cause unintended data loss if not carefully governed. A many-to-many model using two lookup fields on one object has no automatic cascade delete—child records remain (orphaned) when either parent is deleted. The junction object approach is cleaner for true M:M relationships but demands more care around delete permissions and data archiving. The lookup approach provides more flexibility but requires manual cleanup processes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__10", number: 10, question: "What are the implications of using a junction object versus a many-to-many relationship with multiple lookup fields for the sharing model?", answer: "A junction object with two MD parents inherits the most permissive sharing of either parent (the sharing model follows the parent the user has access to). This is useful when access to either parent should grant visibility to the junction record. With lookup fields, the junction object has its own independent OWD and sharing rules, giving architects full control over who sees junction records—important for sensitive data. Lookup-based M:M designs require explicit sharing configuration but avoid unintended access leakage through inheritance."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__11", number: 11, question: "When should an architect use a polymorphic lookup (e.g., WhatId on Task) versus dedicated lookup fields to multiple objects, given cross-object query limitations?", answer: "Polymorphic lookups (like WhatId or WhoId on Activity) allow one field to reference multiple object types, reducing field count and enabling a single record to relate to heterogeneous targets. Use them when the related object type is not known at design time or when modeling generic associations. The limitation is that SOQL on polymorphic fields cannot use standard cross-object field traversal (you cannot use WHERE What.Name in a standard query); you must use TYPEOF in SOQL or handle polymorphism in Apex. Dedicated lookup fields offer cleaner SOQL and better indexing but increase field count and create a separate field per target object."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__12", number: 12, question: "When should a Person Account model be chosen over a standard Account/Contact model for a B2C business?", answer: "Person Accounts merge Account and Contact into a single record, making them ideal for B2C scenarios where the customer is always an individual (e.g., retail, healthcare, financial services) and there is no meaningful company/person distinction. Choose Person Accounts when: the business always sells to individuals, not businesses; there is no need to associate multiple contacts to a single account; and the org is not heavily integrated with B2B processes. Avoid if the org serves both B2B and B2C, because Person Accounts are hard to distinguish from business accounts in SOQL and can confuse integrations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__13", number: 13, question: "What data model trade-offs does enabling Person Accounts introduce for integrations and reporting?", answer: "Person Accounts create records in both the Account and Contact tables simultaneously, which confuses many ETL tools and integrations that treat them as separate entities. Key trade-offs: (1) Cannot be disabled once enabled—permanent org-level change. (2) SOQL must filter by IsPersonAccount to distinguish individual vs. business accounts. (3) Standard Salesforce APIs return Person Account data on both Account and Contact objects, requiring integration logic to de-duplicate. (4) Some standard related lists (e.g., Opportunities, Cases) behave differently. (5) Reports mixing Person Accounts with standard contacts can produce duplicate rows without careful filtering."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__14", number: 14, question: "What are the constraints on External ID fields (field type limitations, maximum per object, indexed behavior)?", answer: "External ID fields can only be of type: Text, Number, Email, or Auto-Number. Each object supports a maximum of 25 external ID fields (shared with unique field quota). All External ID fields are automatically indexed and enforced as unique (or unique within case-sensitive rules). The index enables fast upserts via the Bulk API and Data Loader. However, the uniqueness constraint applies per field, not across a combination of fields, so composite external keys must be concatenated into a single field or managed in integration logic."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__15", number: 15, question: "How do External ID field constraints shape identity strategy in a data model?", answer: "Since each External ID must be unique per object and supports only certain field types, architects must design a single authoritative source-system key or a synthetic composite key (concatenation of system name + ID) that fits in a Text field. The 25-field limit is typically not a practical constraint, but the uniqueness and type restrictions mean you cannot use multi-part natural keys natively. External IDs are critical for upsert operations during migration and integration—they allow idempotent loads and prevent duplicates. A well-designed identity strategy maps each external system's record ID to a dedicated External ID field on the Salesforce object."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__16", number: 16, question: "How do compound fields (Address and Geolocation) affect SOQL query patterns, indexing capabilities, and data migration compared to individual custom fields storing the same information?", answer: "Compound fields (BillingAddress, Geolocation) are stored as individual sub-fields internally but exposed as a single compound in the API. They cannot be used in SOQL WHERE clauses directly as a compound—you must filter on individual sub-fields (e.g., BillingCity, BillingCountry). Sub-fields like BillingCity are indexed, enabling selective queries. Geolocation fields support DISTANCE and GEOLOCATION functions in SOQL. In data migration, compound fields must be decomposed into their sub-components in the source file or ETL mapping. They cannot be used as External IDs. Custom individual text fields give more flexibility for indexing and SOQL filtering but lose the built-in compound API behavior and map UI integration."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__17", number: 17, question: "How does implicit record sharing through master-detail relationships differ from explicit sharing through sharing rules?", answer: "In a master-detail relationship, child records automatically inherit the sharing settings of the parent—users who can access the parent record can also access all its child records, regardless of the child object's OWD. This is implicit and cannot be overridden. Sharing rules, by contrast, are explicit grants: they extend access to specific groups based on record ownership or criteria, on top of the OWD baseline. Explicit sharing can be more granular (per criteria), but requires configuration and recalculation. Implicit MD sharing cannot be restricted below the parent's access level, which is a key architectural constraint for sensitive child data."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__18", number: 18, question: "What are the architectural implications of master-detail versus sharing rules for sensitive child records?", answer: "If sensitive child records (e.g., salary data, medical records) are in a master-detail relationship, any user with access to the parent also sees the child—there is no way to restrict below parent access. This is a major compliance risk. For sensitive data, architects should use a lookup relationship instead, set the child object's OWD to Private, and then apply explicit sharing rules or Apex managed sharing to grant access only to authorized users. This decouples the child's security from the parent's, allowing fine-grained access control at the cost of losing roll-up summaries and cascade delete."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__19", number: 19, question: "When designing account and opportunity team models, how do Account Teams and Opportunity Teams differ in their effect on record sharing?", answer: "Account Teams grant team members access to the Account and its related Contacts, Opportunities, and Cases, based on the team member's access level (Read Only, Read/Write). Opportunity Teams grant access only to the specific Opportunity record and its related Contacts. Account Teams are broader—one team membership cascades to many related records. Opportunity Teams are scoped to a single deal. When sharing a specific deal with a temporary team member, Opportunity Teams are the right tool. For persistent territory-like access across all related records, Account Teams are more efficient."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__20", number: 20, question: "What are the data model implications of Account Teams and Opportunity Teams for high-volume team membership?", answer: "Each Account Team or Opportunity Team member creates a sharing record in the system (AccountShare or OpportunityShare table). In high-volume orgs with thousands of accounts each having large teams, the sharing table can grow enormous, causing sharing recalculation performance degradation. The platform warning threshold is around 10,000 sharing records per object. Architects should limit team sizes, use role hierarchy or territory management instead of individual team members where possible, and monitor sharing table sizes. Large team memberships can also slow record save operations due to sharing recalculation triggers."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__21", number: 21, question: "What are the differences between Hierarchy Custom Settings and List Custom Settings in terms of data access pattern, governor limits, and appropriate use cases in a data model?", answer: "Hierarchy Custom Settings are profile/user-level overridable settings that cascade down from Org > Profile > User—lower levels override higher ones. They are ideal for user-specific configuration (e.g., feature flags per user/profile). List Custom Settings are simple name-value stores, not hierarchical, accessible by name as a map. Both are cached after first access per request, consuming minimal SOQL (one query). Hierarchy settings are best for behavioral configuration that varies by user segment; List settings are best for reference data or lookup tables (e.g., country codes, tax rates). Neither should store large datasets—use Custom Metadata Types for deployable/versionable configuration."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__22", number: 22, question: "How does the use of external objects and Salesforce Connect change data modeling decisions compared to persisted custom objects for integrating external system data?", answer: "External objects via Salesforce Connect virtualize data from external systems in real time—no storage is consumed in Salesforce. They support lookups (including indirect lookups using External ID fields), can be related to standard/custom objects, and appear in page layouts and related lists. However, they cannot be used in SOQL joins with standard objects, do not support triggers, workflow, roll-up summaries, or Apex DML. Data modeling decisions shift: instead of replicating external data into Salesforce custom objects (which consume storage and require ETL), you reference it in place. This is ideal for read-heavy, real-time data that does not need automation or complex querying inside Salesforce."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__23", number: 23, question: "When designing a data model that spans Sales Cloud and Service Cloud, what field-level and object-level features should be used to ensure proper cross-cloud data visibility?", answer: "Account and Contact objects are shared between Sales and Service Cloud, so OWD and sharing rules on these objects affect both clouds simultaneously. Use Field-Level Security (FLS) via Permission Sets to ensure Sales users cannot see sensitive service case data fields and vice versa. Record Types can differentiate Account or Case records by cloud purpose. Sharing rules should be layered to give Service agents read access to Accounts owned by Sales reps when they need to service customers. Use the Case's Account lookup to tie service data back to the sales customer record. Consider separate page layouts per record type to avoid UI clutter, and use Permission Sets to expose cloud-specific objects (e.g., Entitlements for Service) only to relevant users."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__24", number: 24, question: "How does Field History Tracking (limited to 20 fields per object, 18-month retention) compare to Salesforce Shield Field Audit Trail (up to 60 fields, 10-year retention) for meeting long-term audit requirements in a data model?", answer: "Standard Field History Tracking is free, supports up to 20 fields per object, retains data for 18 months in the FieldHistory related list, and is queryable via SOQL. It is sufficient for short-term audit needs. Shield Field Audit Trail (part of Salesforce Shield) supports up to 60 fields per object, retains data for up to 10 years, and stores history in a Big Object (FieldHistoryArchive). For regulated industries (finance, healthcare) with multi-year retention requirements, Shield FAT is required. Because FieldHistoryArchive is a Big Object, it requires index-based queries using the composite index prefix—standard SOQL joins and aggregates are not supported. Architects must factor in the Shield licensing cost and the Big Object query constraints when designing audit-driven data models.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
