        // Section 2: Given a scenario, recommend approaches and techniques to des (9 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an",
            title: "Given a scenario, recommend approaches and techniques to design a scalable data model that obeys the current security and sharing model",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.60, blue: 0.50),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.96, blue: 0.94),
            icon: "checkmark.shield.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_obj",
                    title: "Given a scenario, recommend approaches and techniques to design a scalable data model that obeys the current security and sharing model",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_1", number: 1, question: "How does defining OWD as Private versus Public Read Only versus Public Read/Write affect the scalability of sharing recalculation in high-volume environments?", answer: "OWD Private requires the platform to maintain explicit sharing records for every access grant beyond the owner and role hierarchy, causing sharing recalculation to process more rows when records are reassigned or sharing rules change. Public Read Only and Public Read/Write eliminate the need for most explicit sharing records because all users inherently have access, dramatically reducing sharing recalculation overhead. In high-volume orgs, moving from Private to Public OWD on non-sensitive objects can significantly improve DML performance and reduce sharing-related timeouts. The trade-off is loss of record-level access control, so this is only appropriate for non-sensitive data."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_2", number: 2, question: "What are the differences between criteria-based sharing rules and ownership-based sharing rules?", answer: "Ownership-based sharing rules grant access based on who owns the record—sharing all records owned by a specific Role, Public Group, or Territory with another group. They are simple and performant. Criteria-based sharing rules grant access based on field values on the record (e.g., share all Cases where Status = \"Escalated\" with the Escalation Team). They are more flexible but more resource-intensive because the platform must evaluate criteria against all records when rules change. Criteria-based rules cannot reference cross-object field values, only fields on the record itself. Both types can only grant access equal to or less than the running user's access level."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_3", number: 3, question: "How do criteria-based and ownership-based sharing rules interact with OWD settings to produce the effective access level for a record?", answer: "OWD is the baseline (floor) access level. Sharing rules can only expand access above the OWD—never restrict below it. The effective access for a user on a record is the most permissive combination of: OWD, role hierarchy inheritance, ownership-based rules, criteria-based rules, manual shares, team membership, and Apex managed sharing. If OWD is Public Read/Write, sharing rules are irrelevant because everyone already has full access. If OWD is Private, sharing rules selectively open access to groups. The platform computes effective access as the union (OR) of all grants, so any single grant of higher access wins."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_4", number: 4, question: "How does the Grant Access Using Hierarchies setting on an object interact with the role hierarchy, and what data model design considerations arise when it must be disabled for sensitive objects?", answer: "By default, \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" allows users above a record owner in the role hierarchy to automatically inherit that owner's access level. Disabling this (only possible on custom objects) means managers do NOT automatically see their subordinates' records—access must be granted explicitly via sharing rules or Apex sharing. This is critical for sensitive objects like HR data or compensation records. The data model implication: you must design explicit sharing rules or Apex managed sharing to provide necessary managerial visibility, and reports/dashboards must be run in the context of a sharing-aware user. Role hierarchy cannot be used as a lazy access mechanism for sensitive objects."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_5", number: 5, question: "How does the depth of parent-child hierarchies in a data model affect sharing rule applicability and inherited access?", answer: "In a master-detail hierarchy, child records inherit the parent's sharing access—so sharing rules on the parent automatically extend to the child. However, sharing rules can only be defined on the parent object; you cannot define sharing rules directly on a detail object. In deeply nested MD hierarchies (grandparent-parent-child), sharing access flows from the topmost parent downward. In lookup-based hierarchies, each object has independent sharing, so deeper hierarchies require separate sharing rule definitions at each level. Architects must map out access flows explicitly, especially when some levels contain sensitive fields and others do not."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_6", number: 6, question: "A company needs to restrict visibility of financial records so that sales reps see only their own accounts but managers see their entire team's. Which combination of OWD settings and sharing rules best supports this while keeping the data model simple?", answer: "Set Account OWD to Private. Enable \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" (default behavior). This automatically gives managers in the role hierarchy visibility into their subordinates' records without any sharing rules. Sales reps see only records they own. No explicit sharing rules are needed for the manager visibility requirement. If financial object (e.g., custom Revenue__c object) has MD to Account, it inherits Account access. If it is a separate custom object with Private OWD, enable Grant Access Using Hierarchies on that object as well. This is the simplest, most performant design—role hierarchy does the work, no criteria-based rules required."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_7", number: 7, question: "A scenario requires that certain sensitive fields are only accessible to users in a specific role. What field-level security and object model techniques should be combined to enforce this without duplicating objects?", answer: "Use Field-Level Security (FLS) via Permission Sets to restrict sensitive fields to only users with the appropriate permission set (mapped to the required role or a subset of users). Do not rely on role alone—roles control record access, not field access. Combine FLS with a Permission Set that is assigned only to authorized users. For additional UI control, use separate page layouts per profile/record type to hide sensitive fields from the layout (though FLS, not layout, is the true security boundary). If Salesforce Shield is available, apply Shield Platform Encryption to fields requiring at-rest encryption. This approach avoids object duplication while layering security controls."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_8", number: 8, question: "When a data model requires record-level security that cannot be achieved through standard sharing rules alone, what are the implications of using a custom sharing object versus Apex managed sharing?", answer: "Apex managed sharing uses the standard Share object (e.g., MyObject__Share) to programmatically insert sharing records with a \"Manual\" or custom share cause. It is flexible—any business logic can determine who gets access—but must be maintained carefully: when ownership changes, Apex sharing with share cause \"Apex\" persists, while \"Manual\" shares are deleted on ownership transfer. Custom sharing objects (separate junction objects that model access) require more custom code to translate into actual Salesforce sharing but give more control over the data model. Apex managed sharing is the standard approach; it integrates with the platform's sharing model and is visible in the sharing detail of a record. Both approaches add complexity and require testing with large data volumes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_recommend_approaches_an_9", number: 9, question: "When designing a data model for a community (Experience Cloud) with millions of external users, how should object OWD settings be configured to avoid sharing performance degradation?", answer: "For Experience Cloud with millions of external users, avoid Private OWD on high-volume objects because sharing recalculation for external users is extremely expensive at scale. Prefer Public Read Only or Public Read/Write OWD for objects that community users need to access broadly, and rely on object-level permissions (profile/permission set) to control which object types community users can access at all. For objects requiring record-level restriction, use Controlled by Parent (via MD relationship) rather than Private OWD with sharing rules. Use Customer Community (high-volume) user licenses, which bypass the standard sharing model entirely. These users see records they own or that share a foreign key with their Account/Contact, making sharing calculation irrelevant. Configure sharing sets on the community profile to grant access based on the Account or Contact lookup on the user's record.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 3: Compare and contrast various techniques, approaches and cons (7 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques_",
            title: "Compare and contrast various techniques, approaches and considerations for capturing and managing business and technical metadata",
            color: Color(red: 0.55, green: 0.35, blue: 0.75),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.90, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "gearshape.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__obj",
                    title: "Compare and contrast various techniques, approaches and considerations for capturing and managing business and technical metadata",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__1", number: 1, question: "How should a business data dictionary be maintained in relation to a Salesforce org's object and field metadata to ensure shared understanding across business and technical teams?", answer: "A business data dictionary maps technical metadata (API names, field types, object relationships) to business-friendly definitions, ownership, and usage context. It should reference the Salesforce object's API name and field API name directly so there is a traceable link. Tools like Salesforce's built-in field description, the Metadata API, or third-party data catalog tools (e.g., Alation, Collibra) can auto-populate technical attributes. The business team should own the business definitions and data stewards should keep them current. Automated scripts (Metadata API or Tooling API) can extract the current object/field inventory and flag discrepancies with the dictionary, enabling drift detection."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__2", number: 2, question: "How do custom metadata types serve as technical metadata containers for configuration-driven behavior?", answer: "Custom Metadata Types (CMT) store structured configuration as metadata records that are included in packages, change sets, and version control. Unlike custom settings, CMT records are deployable and versionable, making them ideal for configuration that must move between environments. They can be queried in Apex (with caching), referenced in formula fields, validation rules, and flows. By storing business rules, thresholds, or routing logic in CMT, architects create a configuration layer that admins can modify post-deployment without code changes—reducing the need for metadata deployments for purely behavioral changes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__3", number: 3, question: "How does Salesforce field-level metadata (description, help text, data classification) support data governance and operational documentation within the platform?", answer: "Each Salesforce field has a Description (internal developer notes), Help Text (user-facing tooltip), and data classification attributes (Sensitivity Level, Compliance Categorization, Data Owner). Populating these consistently creates an in-platform data dictionary that is always in sync with the actual schema—unlike external spreadsheet-based dictionaries that drift. Data classification metadata (PII, Sensitive, etc.) drives Privacy Center automation and can be queried via the Tooling API for compliance reporting. Mandating that all custom fields have a populated Description is a foundational governance practice that reduces onboarding time for new developers and admins."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__4", number: 4, question: "What is the role of data classification metadata in Salesforce (Sensitivity Level, Compliance Categorization, Data Owner fields) and how does it integrate with Shield Platform Encryption and Privacy Center?", answer: "Data classification fields on Salesforce field metadata allow architects to tag fields with their sensitivity (PII, Confidential, Internal, Public) and compliance category (GDPR, HIPAA, PCI). Shield Platform Encryption uses these classifications to guide which fields should be encrypted at rest. Privacy Center reads these classifications to drive data subject access request (DSAR) workflows and retention policies. Third-party tools (e.g., Securiti, OneTrust) can also consume these metadata attributes. Data classification in metadata provides a machine-readable governance layer that enables automated policy enforcement without manual tagging at the record level."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__5", number: 5, question: "How does data classification in Salesforce field metadata support downstream governance processes?", answer: "Once fields are classified (e.g., PII, Sensitive), the Privacy Center can use these classifications to automatically identify which fields to include in Data Subject Access Request (DSAR) exports, apply data retention and deletion policies, and generate data inventory reports. Shield Platform Encryption can be selectively applied to classified fields. Data Loss Prevention (DLP) tools integrated with Salesforce Event Monitoring can alert when classified fields are accessed in bulk. Classification also supports audit responses—when a regulator asks \"where is PII stored?\", the classification metadata provides an authoritative, queryable answer via the Tooling API."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__6", number: 6, question: "When designing a taxonomy for a Salesforce data model shared across multiple business units, what techniques help enforce consistent categorization without restricting future extensibility?", answer: "Use a controlled vocabulary stored in Custom Metadata Types or a dedicated Taxonomy custom object for category values, rather than hard-coded picklist values per object. This allows new categories to be added centrally and referenced across multiple objects via lookup. Use record types to differentiate business-unit-specific views without forking the core taxonomy. Apply picklist value sets (Global Picklists) for shared categorical fields so all objects reference the same master list and changes propagate automatically. Version the taxonomy in a CMT record with an \"active\" flag to allow new categories to be introduced without breaking existing ones."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_compare_and_contrast_various_techniques__7", number: 7, question: "How does data lineage tracking in a Salesforce data model help with regulatory compliance and impact analysis when object schemas evolve?", answer: "Data lineage documents where each data element originated (source system), how it was transformed, and where it is consumed (downstream systems, reports, automations). In Salesforce, lineage is typically tracked externally (in a data catalog or lineage tool) because the platform does not natively provide field-level lineage. During schema evolution (e.g., renaming or retiring a field), lineage analysis identifies all reports, formulas, flows, Apex classes, integrations, and dashboards that reference the field—enabling safe, coordinated changes. For GDPR/CCPA, lineage shows that all PII fields are accounted for and demonstrates the data flow to regulators. Lack of lineage documentation makes impact analysis after schema changes error-prone and risky.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 4: Compare and contrast the different reasons for implementing  (4 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s4_compare_and_contrast_the_different_reaso",
            title: "Compare and contrast the different reasons for implementing Big Objects versus standard/custom objects within a production instance",
            color: Color(red: 0.80, green: 0.40, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.94, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "chart.bar.xaxis.ascending",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s4_compare_and_contrast_the_different_reaso_obj",
                    title: "Compare and contrast the different reasons for implementing Big Objects versus standard/custom objects within a production instance",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_compare_and_contrast_the_different_reaso_1", number: 1, question: "What are the primary use cases that justify implementing a Big Object over a custom object, specifically regarding storage capacity, query behavior, and data archiving needs?", answer: "Big Objects are designed for storing billions of records with high durability and no impact on standard org storage limits (they have a separate, very large storage allocation). Primary use cases: (1) Long-term data archiving—storing historical records (e.g., old cases, interactions) that must be retained for compliance but rarely queried. (2) Immutable audit logs—recording every system event at scale. (3) Interaction history—storing every customer touchpoint (emails, clicks) at volume. Standard/custom objects are limited by storage quotas, support rich SOQL, triggers, and automations. Big Objects trade those capabilities for virtually unlimited scale and long-term retention."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_compare_and_contrast_the_different_reaso_2", number: 2, question: "What are the limitations of Big Objects compared to standard custom objects in terms of supported SOQL features, triggers, workflow, and process automation?", answer: "Big Objects do NOT support: standard SOQL (only a restricted subset requiring the composite index prefix), triggers (before/after insert/update/delete), workflow rules, process builder, flows, standard reports and dashboards, list views, or standard Apex DML update/delete. Records are effectively immutable after insert—they cannot be updated. Deletion is supported via two mechanisms: Bulk API (recommended for high-volume hard deletes) and programmatically via Database.deleteImmediate() in Apex for lower-volume or event-driven deletion. They also do not appear in standard related lists. Querying Big Objects requires matching the leading fields of the composite index exactly (equality or range on consecutive index fields in order). This severely limits ad hoc query patterns and makes Big Objects unsuitable for transactional use cases."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_compare_and_contrast_the_different_reaso_3", number: 3, question: "How does the index-based query model of Big Objects (requiring an exact match on the first index field) affect how an architect designs the composite index for anticipated access patterns?", answer: "Big Object queries MUST include an equality filter on the first field of the composite index; subsequent index fields can use equality or range filters but only in the defined order. This means the composite index must be designed around the most common query pattern. Example: if the most frequent query is \"all interactions for a given Contact in a date range,\" the index should be [ContactId, InteractionDate]. If you later need to query by date alone, you cannot—you would need a separate index definition or a separate Big Object. Architects must commit to the primary access pattern upfront because the index is fixed at design time and cannot be easily changed on a populated Big Object."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_compare_and_contrast_the_different_reaso_4", number: 4, question: "What are the data ingestion mechanisms available for populating Big Objects (Apex, CSV, Bulk API) and what constraints apply to each?", answer: "Big Objects can be populated via: (1) Apex insertImmediate() method—synchronous, suitable for real-time inserts from triggers or flows, subject to governor limits. (2) Bulk API—for large-volume batch loads; supports CSV format; recommended for migrations. (3) SOAP API insert—limited and less common. Records cannot be updated once inserted (immutable). Deletion is supported via two mechanisms: Bulk API (hard delete, recommended for high-volume scenarios) and Database.deleteImmediate() in Apex (suitable for programmatic or event-driven deletion of individual records). There is no UI-based data entry for Big Objects. During Apex-based inserts, the 150 DML statement limit and 10,000 records per transaction limits apply, so batch Apex with chunked inserts is the standard pattern for high-volume real-time ingestion.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 5: Given a customer scenario, recommend approaches and techniqu (8 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend approaches and techniques to avoid data skew",
            color: Color(red: 0.25, green: 0.55, blue: 0.70),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.95, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "globe",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend approaches and techniques to avoid data skew",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_1", number: 1, question: "What is the difference between record-locking skew (caused by DML contention on a parent record with many children) and sharing calculation skew (caused by ownership concentration)?", answer: "Record-locking skew occurs when too many child records in a master-detail relationship are updated concurrently, causing the platform to lock the parent record for roll-up recalculation, resulting in \"unable to lock row\" errors. It is a DML performance problem. Sharing calculation skew occurs when one user or queue owns a disproportionately large number of records; any sharing recalculation event (OWD change, sharing rule change, ownership transfer) must process an enormous number of sharing rows for that owner, causing timeouts. These are distinct problems requiring different mitigations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_2", number: 2, question: "How does having more than 10,000 child records under a single parent account affect DML performance and sharing recalculation?", answer: "Salesforce recommends keeping child records per parent below roughly 10,000. Beyond this threshold, roll-up summary recalculations on the parent become increasingly slow, and concurrent DML on children causes parent lock contention. For sharing, if the parent Account's owner changes, all related child records (Contacts, Opportunities, Cases) must have their sharing recalculated. With 50,000+ children this can timeout and fail. The platform may defer sharing recalculation asynchronously in this scenario, meaning users may temporarily see stale access. Architects should distribute records across multiple parent accounts or use a hierarchy of accounts to keep child counts manageable."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_3", number: 3, question: "How does having many records with the same lookup value contribute to record locking contention during concurrent DML operations?", answer: "When many records share the same lookup value (e.g., all Opportunities pointing to a single Corporate Account), concurrent saves of those child records may all trigger sharing recalculation on the same parent simultaneously, creating a contention point. Even without roll-up summaries, the sharing row for the parent account must be read/written during recalculation. Additionally, if there is a roll-up summary or any trigger logic that reads the parent, the parent row is locked. High concurrency plus a shared parent equals a high probability of lock errors. Mitigation: spread records across multiple parent records using a hierarchy or partitioning strategy."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_4", number: 4, question: "How does ownership skew (too many records owned by a single user or group) impact sharing rule performance, and what design patterns mitigate this?", answer: "If one user or queue owns millions of records, any sharing recalculation (triggered by OWD change, sharing rule modification, or ownership change) must process millions of rows for that single owner, causing timeouts and deferred recalculation. Mitigation patterns: (1) Distribute ownership across multiple users using queues with round-robin assignment. (2) Use Public OWD for the skewed object to eliminate the need for sharing rows. (3) Move to role hierarchy-based access so that sharing recalculation is handled via hierarchy rather than explicit rules. (4) For integration accounts, create multiple integration user accounts in the role hierarchy to distribute record ownership."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_5", number: 5, question: "How does the mitigation strategy differ for record-locking skew versus sharing calculation skew?", answer: "Record-locking skew is mitigated by reducing concurrent DML on shared parent records: remove roll-up summary fields (compute aggregates asynchronously), use lookup instead of master-detail (no parent lock for roll-ups), batch DML operations to avoid concurrency, and partition child records across multiple parents. Sharing calculation skew is mitigated by reducing the number of records owned by a single user: distribute ownership, use Public OWD (no sharing rows needed), use role hierarchy instead of explicit sharing rules, or use Customer Community (high-volume) users who bypass the standard sharing model entirely. The root cause differs (DML contention vs. sharing table size) so the solutions are different."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_6", number: 6, question: "When a customer's data model results in excessive child-to-parent relationships through roll-up summary fields, what approaches mitigate lock contention while preserving aggregate visibility?", answer: "Replace synchronous roll-up summary fields with asynchronous aggregate calculation: (1) Use Apex Batch or scheduled jobs to compute and store aggregate values in a persisted field on the parent. (2) Use Platform Events to trigger asynchronous recalculation when child records change. (3) Use a separate Aggregate__c object to store pre-computed summaries, updated by a nightly batch. (4) Evaluate third-party roll-up tools (e.g., DLRS - Declarative Lookup Rollup Summaries) which can run asynchronously. These approaches decouple child saves from parent updates, eliminating the synchronous lock, while still making aggregates available for reporting and UI display, just with a slight delay rather than real-time updates."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_7", number: 7, question: "A large enterprise org has a single corporate account with 50,000 child opportunity records. What architectural techniques should be recommended to mitigate data skew and sharing calculation delays?", answer: "Recommended techniques: (1) Introduce a hierarchy of sub-accounts (e.g., by division or region) to distribute the 50,000 opportunities across multiple parent accounts, keeping each below the 10,000 child threshold. (2) Remove or replace roll-up summary fields on the corporate account with async aggregate calculations. (3) Switch the Opportunity-to-Account relationship to a lookup (not MD) if roll-ups are removed, eliminating parent lock during child DML. (4) If the corporate account must own records, use a queue or multiple integration users to own opportunities rather than one user, distributing sharing row load. (5) If OWD is Private, consider raising it to Public Read Only for Opportunities to eliminate sharing row creation entirely for this non-sensitive data."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_8", number: 8, question: "How does using a private OWD with many criteria-based sharing rules on a skewed object compare to using a public read/write OWD in terms of sharing recalculation performance?", answer: "Private OWD with many criteria-based sharing rules is the worst-case scenario for sharing performance: every record must be evaluated against every rule, and sharing rows must be created for every qualifying record-group pair. With a skewed object (many records per owner or many records matching rule criteria), recalculation is extremely slow and often deferred asynchronously. Public Read/Write OWD eliminates the need for sharing rows entirely. No recalculation is needed because everyone has access. The performance difference can be orders of magnitude. If data sensitivity allows it, raising OWD to Public Read Only or Public Read/Write on skewed objects is the most impactful single change to improve sharing performance.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 6: Compare and contrast the various techniques, approaches and  (8 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq",
            title: "Compare and contrast the various techniques, approaches and considerations for implementing MDM solutions",
            color: Color(red: 0.70, green: 0.30, blue: 0.40),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.90, blue: 0.92),
            icon: "person.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_obj",
                    title: "Compare and contrast the various techniques, approaches and considerations for implementing MDM solutions",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_1", number: 1, question: "What are the four primary MDM implementation styles (Registry, Consolidation, Coexistence, Centralized)?", answer: "Registry: Salesforce acts as an index, storing cross-reference IDs to source systems without storing the master data itself. Source systems remain authoritative; Salesforce provides a lookup service. Consolidation: Data from multiple sources is copied into Salesforce for reporting/analysis, but source systems remain the systems of record for updates. Coexistence: Salesforce stores a \"best version\" master record and shares it back to source systems; both Salesforce and source systems can update data with synchronization. Centralized (Hub): Salesforce is the sole system of record; all systems read from and write to Salesforce. Each style trades off control, data latency, integration complexity, and system-of-record clarity."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_2", number: 2, question: "How does a canonical data model differ from a source-specific data model, and why is it important for harmonizing data from multiple systems into a Salesforce org?", answer: "A source-specific model mirrors the structure of a single source system (e.g., SAP's customer table). A canonical data model is a neutral, agreed-upon standard structure that all source systems map TO during integration, independent of any single source's format. In Salesforce MDM, the canonical model is typically represented by the standard Account/Contact object schema, augmented with custom fields. Without a canonical model, each integration point creates bespoke mappings that become unmaintainable at scale. A canonical model ensures that when two sources send \"customer data,\" they map to the same Salesforce fields using the same field definitions, enabling consistent deduplication and reporting."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_3", number: 3, question: "When choosing between a Coexistence MDM style and a Centralized Hub style for a company that needs Salesforce as its system of record while keeping existing ERP customer data authoritative for financials, what are the key trade-offs?", answer: "Coexistence: Salesforce and ERP coexist as partial systems of record for different attributes. Salesforce owns CRM attributes (contacts, opportunities); ERP owns financial attributes (billing, credit). Data flows bidirectionally. Trade-off: complex conflict resolution when both systems update the same attribute; requires robust synchronization and survivorship rules. Centralized Hub: Salesforce is the single system of record for all customer data; ERP reads from Salesforce. Trade-off: requires migrating ERP's customer master into Salesforce and changing ERP processes to read from Salesforce—high transformation cost and risk. Coexistence is more pragmatic when ERP financial processes cannot easily be re-pointed to Salesforce, but introduces ongoing synchronization complexity."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_4", number: 4, question: "A company integrates customer data from CRM, ERP, and e-commerce. What survivorship rules and weighting strategies should be applied to determine the authoritative attribute value when sources conflict?", answer: "Define survivorship rules per attribute: (1) Most Recent Wins: use the source with the latest LastModifiedDate for that specific attribute. Good for frequently updated fields like email. (2) Most Trusted Source: rank sources by reliability for a given attribute (e.g., ERP is most trusted for billing address; CRM is most trusted for phone). (3) Most Complete: prefer the source with a non-null value; if multiple sources have values, apply another rule. (4) Frequency Wins: if 3 of 5 sources agree on a value, use it. In practice, combine these: define a priority matrix per attribute (e.g., BillingAddress: ERP > CRM > e-commerce; Email: e-commerce > CRM > ERP) and apply Most Recent as a tiebreaker."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_5", number: 5, question: "What matching and de-duplication strategies are available in Salesforce (Duplicate Rules, Matching Rules, third-party MDM) and when is each appropriate for consolidating customer master data?", answer: "Salesforce Matching Rules: define the algorithm for comparing records (exact match, fuzzy match on name, email, phone). Duplicate Rules: use Matching Rules to either block or alert on duplicates during record creation/edit. Duplicate Jobs (Data.com Clean or Apex): batch identification of existing duplicates. These native tools are suitable for moderate-volume duplicate management with standard matching logic. For complex MDM scenarios (multiple systems, probabilistic matching, large-scale deduplication, survivorship automation), third-party MDM platforms (Informatica MDM, Reltio, Talend) provide more sophisticated algorithms, match scores, and golden record assembly pipelines. Third-party MDM is preferred when native tools cannot handle match complexity or volume."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_6", number: 6, question: "How should hierarchy management (account hierarchies, organizational hierarchies) be modeled in Salesforce to support MDM use cases such as roll-up reporting and targeted marketing?", answer: "Use the Account's built-in ParentId self-lookup to model company hierarchies (ultimate parent > subsidiary > division). The Ultimate Parent Account can be identified by walking the ParentId chain. For roll-up reporting across the hierarchy, since standard roll-ups do not work on self-lookups, use: (1) Scheduled Apex Batch jobs to aggregate metrics upward through the hierarchy and store on the ultimate parent record. (2) Reports with custom hierarchy logic. (3) Third-party tools that support hierarchical roll-ups. For targeted marketing, store \"hierarchy role\" metadata (e.g., Ultimate Parent, Regional HQ, Operating Entity) as a custom field to enable segmentation without traversing the hierarchy at query time."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_7", number: 7, question: "When leveraging external reference data (e.g., D&B firmographic data) for account enrichment in Salesforce, what approaches ensure the enrichment does not overwrite cleaner local data?", answer: "Implement field-level survivorship at the enrichment layer: before overwriting a Salesforce field with external data, compare the external value against the existing Salesforce value. Overwrite only if: (1) the Salesforce field is null (fill blank approach), (2) the external source has a higher confidence score for that attribute, or (3) the external data is more recent. Store the source system and timestamp of each enriched field value in companion metadata fields (e.g., AnnualRevenue_Source__c, AnnualRevenue_UpdatedDate__c). Use Custom Metadata Types to configure which fields allow enrichment overwrite vs. protect-local-value rules. This prevents high-quality CRM data from being degraded by lower-quality external reference data."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s6_compare_and_contrast_the_various_techniq_8", number: 8, question: "How does integrating a third-party MDM platform (e.g., Informatica MDM, Reltio) with Salesforce via API differ architecturally from implementing MDM natively within Salesforce, and when is the external MDM approach preferred?", answer: "External MDM via API: the MDM platform is the system of record for master data. Salesforce subscribes to it, receiving golden records via REST/SOAP API or event streaming (e.g., Kafka). The MDM platform handles matching, survivorship, hierarchy, and lineage. Salesforce stores the result. Native Salesforce MDM: uses Duplicate/Matching Rules, Apex, and custom objects to manage master data within the org. External MDM is preferred when: multiple non-Salesforce systems (ERP, data warehouse, legacy CRM) also need master data from the same hub; matching complexity exceeds Salesforce native capabilities; probabilistic matching or ML-based deduplication is required; or a single MDM governance team serves multiple platforms. Native MDM is simpler and lower cost for Salesforce-only scenarios.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 7: Given a customer scenario, recommend and use techniques for  (5 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s7_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_and_",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend and use techniques for establishing a golden record or system of truth for the customer domain in a single org",
            color: Color(red: 0.40, green: 0.60, blue: 0.30),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.96, blue: 0.90),
            icon: "leaf.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s7_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_and__obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend and use techniques for establishing a golden record or system of truth for the customer domain in a single org",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_and__1", number: 1, question: "What are the trade-offs between using a Registry-style MDM pattern (Salesforce as index) versus a Consolidation-style pattern (Salesforce as golden record store) for a customer with real-time data update requirements?", answer: "Registry style: Salesforce stores only cross-reference IDs linking to source system records. Real-time queries must call source systems via API to retrieve actual attribute values—high latency, dependent on source system availability. Simple to implement, low storage, but poor for real-time UI display of consolidated data. Consolidation style: Salesforce stores a physical copy of the golden record, updated periodically or via events. Real-time reads are fast (local query). Updates require replication from source systems with latency equal to the replication frequency. For real-time update requirements, Consolidation with event-driven synchronization (CDC, Platform Events, or streaming API) is preferred—it gives fast reads while keeping data reasonably current."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_and__2", number: 2, question: "When implementing a golden record strategy in Salesforce, how should external IDs from source systems be stored to maintain traceability while the master record serves as the single system of truth?", answer: "Create dedicated External ID fields on the golden record object for each source system (e.g., ERP_Customer_ID__c, MarketingCloud_ContactKey__c, Ecommerce_UserID__c). Mark each as External ID and Unique to enable upsert operations and fast lookup. Store them alongside the Salesforce record ID, which is the golden record's own identifier. Optionally, create a separate Cross-Reference object (CrossRef__c) with fields for SourceSystem__c, ExternalID__c, and a lookup to the master record—this is more flexible for unlimited source systems but requires an extra object and query. The External ID field approach is simpler and searchable directly on the Account/Contact record."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_and__3", number: 3, question: "How do Salesforce Duplicate Management features (matching rules, duplicate rules, duplicate jobs) support the creation and maintenance of a golden record within a single org?", answer: "Matching Rules define the criteria for identifying potential duplicates (e.g., same Email AND similar Name). Duplicate Rules determine what happens when a match is found: Alert (warn user, allow save) or Block (prevent save). Duplicate Jobs proactively scan existing records and generate a Duplicate Record Set of matches. Together, they prevent new duplicates from entering the org (Duplicate Rules) and surface existing duplicates for resolution (Duplicate Jobs). For golden record maintenance, configure Duplicate Rules to block creation of records that match an existing master, and use Duplicate Jobs periodically to identify drift. After identifying duplicates, use the Merge API or standard UI merge to consolidate into the surviving golden record."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_and__4", number: 4, question: "When Salesforce merges duplicate Account records using the standard merge UI or Merge API, what happens to the child records, related files, and custom sharing grants of the non-surviving records, and how does this affect golden record maintenance?", answer: "During a merge: (1) All child records (Contacts, Opportunities, Cases, etc.) of the losing records are reparented to the winning (master) record. (2) Files and attachments (ContentDocument links) from losing records are associated with the winning record. (3) Manual shares on the losing records are deleted; only the winning record's shares remain. (4) Activity history is merged. (5) The losing records are permanently deleted. For golden record maintenance: ensure the winning record ID is the designated golden record ID (shared with integrated systems). After merge, update integrated systems with the winning Salesforce ID via event or API callback. Custom sharing grants must be re-applied to the winning record if the losing record had specific shares that need to be preserved."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s7_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_and__5", number: 5, question: "A single Salesforce org receives customer updates from both a marketing automation platform and a call center CRM. How should a golden record be established and maintained when these systems have conflicting address data?", answer: "Establish a field-level survivorship policy: define which system is most trusted for address data (e.g., call center CRM is more trusted because agents verify addresses during calls). Implement: (1) Source-stamping fields (Address_Source__c, Address_LastUpdated__c) on the Account/Contact to track which system last wrote the address. (2) Integration middleware (MuleSoft, Boomi) or Apex logic that compares incoming address values against the current value and applies the survivorship rule before writing. (3) If the marketing platform sends an address update, check if the call center has updated more recently—if yes, ignore the marketing update for the address fields. Use CMT-based configuration to adjust survivorship rules without code changes.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 8: Given a customer scenario, recommend approaches and techniqu (3 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s8_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend approaches and techniques for consolidating data attributes from multiple sources",
            color: Color(red: 0.50, green: 0.30, blue: 0.60),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.90, blue: 0.96),
            icon: "cpu.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s8_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend approaches and techniques for consolidating data attributes from multiple sources",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_1", number: 1, question: "When consolidating contact data from three source systems, how would you define attribute-level survivorship criteria such as most recent, most complete, most trusted source, and frequency-wins?", answer: "Define survivorship per attribute in a matrix: Most Recent: compare LastModifiedDate or a source-stamped update timestamp per attribute; use the most recently updated value. Most Complete: if one source has a value and others do not, the non-null value wins. Most Trusted Source: rank source systems by reliability for each attribute (e.g., System A is most trusted for email, System B for phone). Frequency Wins: if 2 out of 3 systems agree on a value, that value wins. In practice: apply Most Trusted first, then Most Recent as a tiebreaker, then Most Complete as a fallback for nulls. Document this logic in a survivorship rule matrix stored in CMT for auditability and easy updates."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_2", number: 2, question: "What role does source system confidence scoring play in multi-source attribute consolidation, and how can this be implemented using Salesforce field values and metadata?", answer: "Confidence scoring assigns a numeric reliability score to each source system for each attribute type (e.g., System A has 95% confidence for email, 60% for phone). When multiple sources provide a value, the source with the highest confidence score wins. Implementation: create a Custom Metadata Type (SourceSystemConfig__mdt) with fields: SourceSystemName__c, AttributeType__c, ConfidenceScore__c. In integration Apex logic, retrieve the CMT config to determine which source wins for each incoming attribute. Store the winning source name and confidence score on companion fields (e.g., Email_SourceSystem__c, Email_Confidence__c) so the scoring decision is auditable at the record level."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s8_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_3", number: 3, question: "A customer has phone numbers from 5 different systems with varying data quality. What methodology determines the winning phone number attribute when survivorship rules produce ties?", answer: "When survivorship rules tie (e.g., two systems have equal confidence scores and equally recent updates for phone): (1) Apply a pre-defined tiebreaker priority list (e.g., System A > System B > System C regardless of other factors). (2) Apply data quality validation: score each value against format validation (e.g., correct digit count, valid country code), and prefer the value that passes more validation checks. (3) Frequency wins: if 3 of 5 systems provide the same phone number, use it. (4) If still tied, flag the record for manual steward review via a Task or case. Documenting the tiebreaker logic in a CMT ensures it is transparent, configurable, and auditable—critical for regulated environments where data provenance matters.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 9: Given a customer scenario, recommend appropriate approaches  (3 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s9_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend appropriate approaches and techniques to capture and maintain customer reference and metadata to preserve traceability and establish a common context for business rules",
            color: Color(red: 0.60, green: 0.50, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.96, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "lightbulb.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s9_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend appropriate approaches and techniques to capture and maintain customer reference and metadata to preserve traceability and establish a common context for business rules",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_1", number: 1, question: "How should source system cross-references (cross-reference IDs linking Salesforce records to originating system records) be stored and maintained to support bidirectional traceability?", answer: "For a small number of source systems, store each system's ID as a dedicated External ID field on the Salesforce record (e.g., SAP_Customer_ID__c, Oracle_Party_ID__c). For many source systems, use a dedicated Cross-Reference object (CrossRef__c) with fields: SalesforceRecordId__c (lookup to master), SourceSystem__c (picklist), ExternalID__c (text, External ID), and LastSyncDate__c. This supports unlimited source systems and querying all cross-refs for a given master record. Maintain cross-references by updating them during every sync event—if a source system re-keys a record, update the ExternalID on the cross-ref rather than creating a new master record. This enables bidirectional lookups: Salesforce ID to source ID and source ID to Salesforce ID."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_2", number: 2, question: "What approaches allow business rules that depend on customer segment metadata (e.g., enterprise vs. SMB) to remain consistent across multiple systems when the customer's classification is managed in Salesforce?", answer: "Designate Salesforce as the system of record for customer segment classification. Publish segment changes to downstream systems via: (1) Change Data Capture (CDC)—downstream systems subscribe to Account CDC events and update their local segment field. (2) Platform Events—publish a custom CustomerSegmentChanged__e event when the segment field changes, consumed by integration middleware. (3) API polling—downstream systems periodically query Salesforce for segment changes using a LastModifiedDate watermark. Store the classification in a standard field (e.g., Account.Type or a custom Segment__c field) with a controlled picklist to ensure consistent values. Use CMT to define segment-specific business rule parameters that consuming systems can also retrieve."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s9_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_3", number: 3, question: "When a customer record in Salesforce was created by merging records from multiple systems, how should the provenance metadata be preserved to support audit requirements and future re-processing?", answer: "After a merge, the surviving golden record should carry provenance metadata: (1) Store all source system IDs in cross-reference records (CrossRef__c) linked to the master—do not discard the losing records' external IDs. (2) Create a MergeEvent__c custom object recording: merge date, user/process that performed the merge, IDs of merged records, and survivorship decisions made. (3) Tag the golden record with a flag (e.g., IsConsolidated__c = true) and a count of merged sources. (4) Use Shield Field Audit Trail or custom history tracking on key master fields to show the pre-merge values. Document the tiebreaker logic in CMT for auditability and easy updates.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 10: Given a customer scenario, recommend appropriate combination (4 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s10_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend appropriate combination of Salesforce license types to effectively leverage standard and custom objects to meet business needs",
            color: Color(red: 0.35, green: 0.45, blue: 0.65),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.92, blue: 0.96),
            icon: "building.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s10_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend appropriate combination of Salesforce license types to effectively leverage standard and custom objects to meet business needs",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_1", number: 1, question: "What are the data access differences between Salesforce Platform licenses and full Salesforce licenses, and how does this affect which standard objects can be used in a data model?", answer: "Full Salesforce licenses provide access to all standard objects (Account, Contact, Lead, Opportunity, Case, Campaign, etc.) and unlimited custom objects. Salesforce Platform licenses provide access to Account, Contact, and custom objects only. No access to Leads, Opportunities, Cases, Campaigns, or other Sales/Service Cloud standard objects. This means a data model for Platform-licensed users cannot rely on Opportunity or Case for its core processes; those business processes must be modeled in custom objects. Architects must identify which standard objects each user population needs access to and select licenses accordingly, since using the wrong license type results in users being unable to access necessary records."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_2", number: 2, question: "How do Experience Cloud license types (Customer Community, Customer Community Plus, Partner Community) differ in their access to standard Salesforce objects, and what does this mean for data model design?", answer: "Customer Community: access to Accounts, Contacts, Cases, and custom objects. Read/write on Cases; limited access to other standard objects. Customer Community Plus: same as Customer Community but adds access to Reports, Dashboards, and sharing rules (supports Apex sharing). Partner Community: access to Accounts, Contacts, Opportunities, Leads, and custom objects. Full Sales Cloud object visibility. Data model implications: for a self-service portal (Customer Community), design around Cases and custom objects. For a partner portal that needs pipeline visibility, Partner Community is required because it enables Opportunity access. Community Plus is needed if external users need to see reports or participate in complex sharing scenarios."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_3", number: 3, question: "A company needs to give 500 external partners read access to selected account and case data. Which license type and object visibility strategy minimizes cost while meeting data access requirements?", answer: "Customer Community Plus licenses are the most cost-effective for external users who need Account and Case read access along with the ability to participate in sharing rules. Design: (1) Use Customer Community (high-volume) users if the sharing model can be simplified to ownership or account-based visibility. High-volume community users do not count toward sharing row limits and are the most cost-effective option at scale. (2) Set Account and Case OWD appropriately; use sharing sets (available for high-volume community users) to grant access based on the Account lookup on the user's Contact record. (3) Restrict object visibility via profile object permissions and field-level security to expose only selected fields."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s10_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_appr_4", number: 4, question: "When a customer wants to use Service Cloud functionality for 200 agents and Salesforce Platform licenses for 1,000 internal users who only need account and custom object access, how should the data model be designed to respect license limitations?", answer: "Design a data model where: (1) Core customer data (Account, Contact) is on standard objects—accessible to both Service Cloud and Platform license users. (2) Service-specific processes (Cases, Entitlements, Service Contracts) remain on standard Service Cloud objects—only the 200 agents (Service Cloud licenses) access these. (3) Business processes for the 1,000 Platform users are modeled exclusively on custom objects, accessible under Platform license. (4) Avoid placing critical business data on Opportunity or Case for Platform users—they cannot access it. (5) Use cross-object lookups from custom objects to Account/Contact so Platform users can relate their data to the customer master without needing Service Cloud objects.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 11: Given a customer scenario, recommend techniques to ensure da (5 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s11_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_tech",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend techniques to ensure data is persisted in a consistent manner",
            color: Color(red: 0.30, green: 0.50, blue: 0.80),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.94, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "doc.text.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s11_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_tech_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend techniques to ensure data is persisted in a consistent manner",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_tech_1", number: 1, question: "What Salesforce platform mechanisms (validation rules, Apex triggers, duplicate rules, required fields) should be layered together to enforce data consistency at the point of entry?", answer: "Layer these controls: (1) Required fields (declarative)—enforce mandatory data entry at the UI and API level. (2) Validation rules (declarative)—enforce format, range, and cross-field logical constraints. They fire for UI, API, and most integration channels. (3) Duplicate rules—prevent duplicate record creation based on matching rules. (4) Apex triggers (programmatic)—handle complex cross-object consistency, referential integrity checks, and business rule enforcement that cannot be expressed declaratively. Each layer catches a different class of error. Declarative controls are simpler and more maintainable; use Apex only for logic that cannot be achieved declaratively. All layers apply to all write channels (UI, API, Bulk API with allOrNone=true) unless explicitly bypassed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_tech_2", number: 2, question: "What is the role of Salesforce Duplicate Management's Block versus Alert action in enforcing data consistency at load time versus user entry time, and when should each be used?", answer: "Block action: prevents the record from being saved if a duplicate is detected. Ensures zero duplicates enter the system. Use for strict MDM environments or regulated data where duplicate prevention is mandatory. However, Block can cause failures during bulk data loads if legitimate records are incorrectly flagged. Alert action: saves the record but displays a warning to the user, allowing them to proceed or cancel. Use when duplicate detection is advisory—you want users to be aware but not blocked, allowing human judgment. For load-time (API/Bulk): Alert is generally preferred to avoid batch failures; Block is appropriate only when the integration layer is designed to handle and resolve duplicate matches before retrying."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_tech_3", number: 3, question: "How do transaction control mechanisms in Apex (savepoints, rollbacks) support data consistency when a business process writes to multiple related objects?", answer: "Salesforce Apex supports Database.setSavepoint() and Database.rollback() within a single transaction. If a business process writes to Object A, then Object B, and Object B's write fails, a rollback to the savepoint reverts Object A's changes too—maintaining atomicity. This prevents partial commits (e.g., creating an Order without its Order Line Items). Key constraint: savepoints and rollbacks work only within a single synchronous Apex transaction. They cannot span asynchronous operations (future methods, batch jobs, queueable). For processes spanning async operations, architects must implement compensating transactions or use an idempotent retry pattern to ensure consistency after failure."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_tech_4", number: 4, question: "When multiple integration channels (API, manual UI, bulk load) write to the same Salesforce object, what pattern ensures that validation rules and referential integrity are enforced uniformly regardless of the data entry channel?", answer: "Validation rules and required fields enforce uniformly across all channels (UI, REST API, SOAP API, Bulk API with allOrNone=true) by default. Apex triggers also fire for all channels. The risk channel is Bulk API with allOrNone=false and the \"Bypass Validation Rules\" custom permission or permission-set workarounds. Design pattern: (1) Do not grant \"Customize Application\" or validation bypass permissions to integration users unless absolutely necessary. (2) Use a single Apex trigger handler for all consistency checks—this fires regardless of channel. (3) For external IDs and referential integrity, enforce uniqueness via External ID field constraints rather than relying on application logic. (4) Document and govern all bypass permissions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s11_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_tech_5", number: 5, question: "What are the risks of using the Bulk API with allOrNone=false for maintaining referential integrity when loading related objects such as accounts and their child contacts simultaneously?", answer: "With allOrNone=false, each record in a batch succeeds or fails independently. If some Account records fail to load, the corresponding Contact records that reference those Accounts will also fail (unresolved foreign key). But if Contacts are loaded in a subsequent batch after Accounts, and some Account loads partially failed, the Contact load will partially succeed—creating orphaned Contacts referencing non-existent Accounts if the Account ID mapping is not carefully validated. Risks: (1) Partial loads leave data in an inconsistent intermediate state. (2) Error handling complexity increases because you must correlate failures across related object loads. Mitigation: use allOrNone=true where atomicity is critical, or implement robust error correlation and rollback logic in the ETL layer.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 12: Given a customer scenario, recommend a design to achieve a s (4 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s12_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend a design to achieve a single view of the customer within a single Salesforce instance",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.60, blue: 0.50),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.96, blue: 0.94),
            icon: "checkmark.shield.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s12_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend a design to achieve a single view of the customer within a single Salesforce instance",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_1", number: 1, question: "What Salesforce features and data model patterns support building a single view of the customer within a single org, spanning Sales, Service, and Marketing interactions?", answer: "The Account and Contact objects serve as the canonical customer records. Relate all interaction data (Cases, Opportunities, Campaign Members, Call Logs) to the Contact or Account via lookup or master-detail. Use the Individual object (with DataPrivacy records) for GDPR/consent management linked to Contact. For Marketing Cloud integration, use the Connected Campaigns and Marketing Cloud Connector to link MC data back to the canonical Contact. For a 360-degree UI view, use the Account and Contact page layouts with related lists exposing all interaction objects. Einstein Analytics or CRM Analytics can aggregate cross-object interaction data into a unified customer profile dashboard."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_2", number: 2, question: "What are the trade-offs between persisting a single view of the customer as a materialized record in Salesforce versus computing it on-demand via API calls to source systems?", answer: "Materialized (persisted): fast reads, no dependency on source system availability, supports SOQL queries, reports, and automation. Requires ETL or event-driven sync to keep data current—introduces replication latency and storage costs. Good for data that changes infrequently or where read performance matters. On-demand (virtualized via API): always real-time, no storage cost, no replication lag. But: slow UI load times (API call latency), dependent on source system uptime, cannot be used in SOQL WHERE clauses or reports, consumes API call limits. Good for data that changes very frequently or must be authoritative at the moment of display. Hybrid approach: persist stable attributes, virtualize volatile real-time data (e.g., inventory levels, account balance)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_3", number: 3, question: "When multiple interaction systems each maintain partial customer profiles, how should external IDs be used to link records across systems and maintain a unified view in Salesforce?", answer: "Each source system should have its own External ID field on the Salesforce Account or Contact record (e.g., MarketingCloud_ContactKey__c, ERP_CustomerID__c, ServiceDesk_UserID__c). During integration, source systems send their native ID alongside the customer data. Salesforce uses the External ID field for upsert operations—if the External ID matches an existing record, update it; otherwise, create a new record. This prevents duplicates and ensures each source system's updates are routed to the correct Salesforce record. The Salesforce record ID then becomes the canonical identifier shared back to source systems after the first synchronization, enabling future API calls to reference records by the Salesforce ID directly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s12_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_4", number: 4, question: "How does Salesforce Data Cloud (formerly Customer Data Platform) differ architecturally from a custom unified profile built on standard Salesforce objects for creating a single customer view across interaction systems?", answer: "Data Cloud: purpose-built for ingesting, harmonizing, and unifying data from unlimited sources at massive scale. Uses identity resolution (deterministic and probabilistic matching) to build a Unified Individual profile across sources. Stores data in a separate, high-scale data store (not standard Salesforce storage). Supports real-time segmentation, activation to Marketing Cloud, and Einstein AI features. Custom unified profile on standard objects: built using standard Account/Contact schema, custom fields, and integration logic. Limited by Salesforce storage, SOQL governor limits, and manual identity resolution logic. Data Cloud is preferred when: source data volume exceeds standard org capacity, probabilistic identity resolution is needed, or marketing activation at scale is a requirement. Custom models are lower cost for simpler, lower-volume scenarios.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 13: Given a customer scenario, recommend a design to effectively (6 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s13_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend a design to effectively consolidate and/or leverage data from multiple Salesforce instances",
            color: Color(red: 0.55, green: 0.35, blue: 0.75),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.90, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "gearshape.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s13_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend a design to effectively consolidate and/or leverage data from multiple Salesforce instances",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_1", number: 1, question: "What are the primary patterns (hub-and-spoke, point-to-point, shared middleware) for consolidating data across multiple Salesforce orgs, and what are the trade-offs of each?", answer: "Hub-and-Spoke: one central master org aggregates data from spoke orgs via integration. Simple governance, single source of truth. Trade-off: hub becomes a bottleneck and single point of failure; spoke orgs may have latency in seeing consolidated data. Point-to-Point: each org integrates directly with every other org. Simple for 2 orgs, exponentially complex for N orgs (N*(N-1)/2 connections). Hard to govern and maintain. Shared Middleware (MuleSoft, Boomi, MQ): an integration layer sits between all orgs, routing data and handling transformation. Scales well, decouples orgs, supports complex routing and orchestration. Trade-off: adds infrastructure cost and operational complexity. For 3+ orgs, shared middleware is almost always the right choice."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_2", number: 2, question: "What role does a global external ID strategy play when consolidating records from multiple Salesforce orgs into a single master org?", answer: "When consolidating records from multiple orgs (e.g., Org A and Org B) into a master org, both source orgs may have different Salesforce record IDs for the same real-world entity. A global external ID strategy assigns a globally unique identifier (GUID) to each entity before consolidation—stored as an External ID field in all orgs. During consolidation, the master org uses this GUID as the upsert key to match incoming records from both source orgs to the correct master record, preventing duplicates. Without a global external ID, the only keys are org-specific Salesforce IDs, which are meaningless across orgs and would cause duplicate creation or failed upserts during consolidation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_3", number: 3, question: "How does Salesforce Connect with Cross-Org Adapter differ from real-time API integration for accessing data from another Salesforce org, and when is each preferred?", answer: "Cross-Org Adapter (Salesforce Connect): creates external objects in Org A that proxy data from Org B in real time. No data is stored in Org A—queries flow through to Org B. Relationships can be created between Org A objects and the external objects. No storage consumed. Preferred for: read-heavy reference data that belongs in Org B but needs visibility in Org A, without replication. Limitations: no SOSL, no automation on external objects, OData callout limits apply. Real-time API integration: Org A's Apex or middleware calls Org B's REST/SOAP API to read or write data. More flexible, supports write operations, SOQL-equivalent queries, and complex logic. Preferred for: bidirectional data sync, write operations, or when governance requires data to be physically replicated. API integration has higher implementation complexity but far more capability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_4", number: 4, question: "When Salesforce-to-Salesforce (S2S) connection is used to share records between partner orgs, what are its limitations compared to API-based replication in terms of supported objects, field mapping, and bidirectional sync?", answer: "S2S supports a limited set of standard objects (Accounts, Contacts, Leads, Opportunities, Cases, and some others) and does not support custom objects or all standard objects. Field mapping is restricted to fields that exist in both orgs; custom fields in one org cannot be mapped to custom fields in another without manual configuration. S2S is primarily push-based—one org publishes records and the partner subscribes; true bidirectional sync with conflict resolution is not natively supported. API-based replication via REST/SOAP or middleware supports all objects (standard and custom), all field types, complex transformation logic, bidirectional sync with conflict resolution, and higher data volumes. For modern integration needs, API-based replication via middleware (MuleSoft, Boomi) is almost always preferred over S2S."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_5", number: 5, question: "When a company merges two Salesforce orgs and needs to consolidate Account and Contact records, what strategy prevents duplicate creation and maintains referential integrity for related objects?", answer: "Strategy: (1) Assign global External IDs to all Account and Contact records in both orgs before migration (using a GUID or a composite key from source data). (2) Deduplicate at the source: identify records that exist in both orgs using matching logic (email, name, address); designate the surviving record. (3) Load Accounts first using upsert on External ID—the master org uses the External ID to match and update existing records or create new ones. (4) Load Contacts next, referencing the Account External ID to maintain the Account-Contact relationship. (5) Load child objects (Opportunities, Cases) in dependency order, referencing their parent's External ID. (6) After load, validate record counts and spot-check referential integrity. Run duplicate detection to catch any mismatches."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s13_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_de_6", number: 6, question: "A global enterprise operates Sales Cloud in EMEA and North America as separate orgs. A new requirement demands a unified customer view. What are the architectural trade-offs between org consolidation versus real-time federation?", answer: "Org Consolidation: merge both orgs into one. Provides a true single data model, unified reporting, no federation complexity, and simpler governance. Trade-offs: extremely complex migration (data, configuration, customization, integrations); risk of data loss; significant project cost and timeline; may face data residency challenges (EMEA data in a US-hosted org). Real-time Federation: keep both orgs separate but build a federated view via Data Cloud, a middleware hub, or cross-org Salesforce Connect. Provides unified visibility without migration risk. Trade-offs: ongoing synchronization complexity, potential for divergent data models and governance, latency in unified view. Federation is preferred when data residency regulations require geographic separation or when migration risk is unacceptable. Consolidation is preferred for long-term simplicity and operational efficiency.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 14: Given a customer scenario, recommend an approach for designi (12 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend an approach for designing a GDPR compliant data model",
            color: Color(red: 0.80, green: 0.40, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.94, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "chart.bar.xaxis.ascending",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend an approach for designing a GDPR compliant data model",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_1", number: 1, question: "What Salesforce features (data classification metadata, field audit trail, Shield Platform Encryption) support GDPR compliance?", answer: "Data Classification metadata: tag each field with sensitivity level (PII, Confidential, etc.) and compliance categorization (GDPR, HIPAA). Drives automated governance workflows in Privacy Center. Field Audit Trail (Shield): retains field change history for up to 10 years, supporting GDPR audit requirements for demonstrating when personal data was accessed or modified. Shield Platform Encryption: encrypts PII fields at rest using tenant-managed keys; supports GDPR's data protection \"by design and by default\" requirement. Privacy Center: automates Data Subject Access Requests (DSAR), right-to-erasure workflows, and retention policies. This defense-in-depth approach ensures no single control failure exposes PII."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_2", number: 2, question: "How should data classification labels (e.g., PII, sensitive, confidential) be applied to Salesforce fields, and how does this classification integrate with data masking and access control decisions?", answer: "Apply classification via Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Fields > [Field] > Edit > Data Classification section, or via Metadata API (FieldDefinition). Set ComplianceCategorization (PII, HIPAA, PCI, etc.) and SecurityClassification (Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted). Integration with access control: use classification to drive Permission Set assignment decisions. Restricted fields should have FLS set to Read or Hidden for all standard profiles, with access granted only via Permission Set to authorized roles. Data masking: Privacy Center and third-party tools (e.g., Salesforce Sandbox seeding tools) read classification metadata to mask PII fields in sandbox copies. This creates a machine-readable governance layer that automates policy enforcement without manual, error-prone field-by-field decisions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_3", number: 3, question: "What is the difference between Salesforce Shield Platform Encryption and Classic Encryption, and when should each be recommended for protecting PII fields in a GDPR context?", answer: "Classic Encryption: available in all orgs; encrypts a single custom field type (\"Encrypted Text\", max 175 chars) using AES-128 with an org-managed key, storing the field value encrypted at rest in the database. Users without the \"View Encrypted Data\" permission see a masked value in the UI and API responses. Classic Encryption does provide at-rest encryption for its specific field type. However, it falls short for GDPR compliance for two key reasons: (1) Extremely limited scope—only the special \"Encrypted Text\" custom field type can be encrypted; standard fields (Email, Phone, Name), files, and other field types cannot be protected. (2) No customer-controlled key lifecycle—the encryption key is org-managed by Salesforce, meaning there is no tenant-managed key rotation and no cryptographic erasure capability. Shield Platform Encryption: encrypts data at rest using AES-256 with a tenant secret managed by the customer. Shield decrypts transparently at the database layer, meaning any user with standard Field-Level Security (FLS) access to the field receives plaintext in the UI, exports, and API responses—regardless of any \"View Encrypted Data\" permission. Shield's protection is against infrastructure-level exposure (Salesforce infrastructure, physical backups), not against authorized Salesforce users. For GDPR: Shield is the correct recommendation because it provides at-rest encryption across a wide range of standard and custom field types, with customer-controlled key management and the ability to perform cryptographic erasure by destroying the tenant secret—satisfying GDPR's technical security and right-to-erasure requirements. Classic Encryption is insufficient for GDPR compliance primarily due to its narrow field scope and the absence of customer-managed key lifecycle controls."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_4", number: 4, question: "How should an architect layer data classification, field audit trail, and Shield Platform Encryption in a GDPR-compliant data model?", answer: "Layer 1 - Classification: tag all PII/sensitive fields with GDPR compliance categorization. This creates the inventory and drives all downstream automation. Layer 2 - Shield Platform Encryption: apply encryption to all fields tagged as PII or higher. This fulfills \"data protection by design.\" Shield encrypts at the database storage layer, protecting against infrastructure-level exposure such as unauthorized access to backups or raw data stores. Access control for authorized Salesforce users is governed separately by FLS—Shield does not restrict what authorized users can see or export. Layer 3 - Field-Level Security: restrict access to classified fields via Permission Sets. Only authorized users can see the field values in the UI and API responses. Layer 4 - Field Audit Trail: enable on key PII fields to track who changed what and when, satisfying GDPR accountability and audit requirements. Layer 5 - Privacy Center: configure DSAR workflows using the classification metadata to automatically include all PII fields in subject access exports."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_5", number: 5, question: "How does Shield Platform Encryption key management work, including the tenant secret, key derivation, and what happens to data if an org's key is destroyed or rotated?", answer: "Shield uses a two-factor key derivation: (1) Salesforce generates and manages a Service Master Secret. (2) The customer generates and manages a Tenant Secret (uploaded or generated via Key Management UI). These two secrets are combined (XOR) to derive the actual Data Encryption Key (DEK) used to encrypt data. The customer's Tenant Secret is stored in Salesforce but only the customer can rotate or destroy it. Key rotation: generating a new Tenant Secret causes re-encryption of existing data with the new key. Old data remains accessible during the transition period. Key destruction: if the Tenant Secret is destroyed (and Salesforce's copy deleted via the API), the DEK cannot be derived. All Shield-encrypted data becomes permanently inaccessible (cryptographic erasure). This supports GDPR's right to erasure without deleting records."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_6", number: 6, question: "What are the implications of applying Shield Platform Encryption to fields that are used in SOQL WHERE clauses, reports, and list views, and how do these functional restrictions influence the decision to encrypt?", answer: "Standard Shield encryption (probabilistic) prevents encrypted fields from being used in SOQL WHERE clauses, list view filters, or as report filter criteria. The underlying reason is that the database stores ciphertext and cannot perform equality or range comparisons on encrypted values at the storage layer. Deterministic encryption (a Shield option) allows exact-match SOQL filtering but not range queries or SOSL. It is important to understand that Shield's protection model is storage-layer security: any user with standard FLS access to an encrypted field will receive the plaintext value in query results, exports, and API responses—Shield does not restrict data visibility for authorized users. The SOQL filtering restriction is a technical consequence of encryption, not an access control mechanism. Design implications: do NOT encrypt fields that must be used as SOQL WHERE filters unless deterministic encryption is acceptable. Encrypt only truly sensitive PII fields (SSN, date of birth, financial account numbers) where range filtering is not required. Document the encryption impact on each field's usage before applying encryption, since retrofitting is costly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_7", number: 7, question: "How does the Salesforce Privacy Center feature support GDPR compliance workflows such as data subject access requests and automated data retention and deletion?", answer: "Privacy Center provides: (1) Data Subject Rights Management: automate DSAR (access and portability) by discovering all PII fields tagged with GDPR classification, assembling a data export per subject on request. (2) Right to Erasure: automate deletion or anonymization of a data subject's records across all related objects, respecting configurable retention rules. (3) Retention Policies: define how long different record types are kept; Privacy Center automatically deletes or archives records past their retention period. (4) Data Inventory: auto-generate a data map of where PII is stored based on classification metadata. Privacy Center uses the field classification labels to know which fields constitute personal data. This is why accurate classification is foundational to GDPR automation in Salesforce."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_8", number: 8, question: "A customer requires that personal data be identifiable and exportable on demand (GDPR data portability). What data model and tooling approach supports this requirement in Salesforce?", answer: "Data model requirements: (1) All PII fields must be classified with GDPR compliance categorization. (2) A single Contact/Account record must serve as the subject record that links all related personal data (Cases, Orders, Interactions, Consent records). (3) Cross-reference IDs link the Salesforce record to other systems' data for a complete export. Tooling: Privacy Center automates DSAR assembly using field classification metadata. It traverses the object graph from the subject record and exports all related PII fields. For data not in Salesforce (e.g., Marketing Cloud), the export must be coordinated via API calls to those systems. Provide the exported data in a machine-readable, portable format (CSV or JSON). Test the export workflow end-to-end before going live to ensure completeness."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_9", number: 9, question: "How should the right-to-erasure (GDPR Article 17) requirement be addressed in a Salesforce data model that has audit trail requirements and cross-object relationships referencing the data subject?", answer: "Tension: GDPR requires erasure; audit and legal requirements require record retention. Resolution: (1) Anonymization over deletion: replace PII field values with random tokens or generic values (e.g., \"DELETED_USER\" for name, \"deleted@example.com\" for email), preserving the record shell and its relationships for audit purposes. The record ID and related records remain intact, but personal data is removed. (2) Cryptographic erasure via Shield: destroy the encryption key for the data subject's records. Data becomes unreadable without deleting physical records. (3) If true deletion is required: use Privacy Center to cascade-delete related records, then handle referential integrity breaks (orphaned related records). Document the decision and legal basis for each approach in the governance program."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_10", number: 10, question: "When a data subject invokes the right to be forgotten, what is the difference between anonymizing the data versus deleting the record in Salesforce, and which approach better preserves referential integrity and audit history?", answer: "Anonymization: PII field values are overwritten with non-identifying values (e.g., \"Anonymous\"). The record's Salesforce ID is preserved, so all related records (Cases, Orders, Activity History) continue to reference a valid parent. Referential integrity is maintained. Audit history (Field Audit Trail entries) may still contain the original PII unless also anonymized. Deletion: the physical record is deleted; all child records in MD relationships are cascade-deleted; lookup references from other objects become null or orphaned. Referential integrity breaks, and audit history of related records may lose context. Anonymization is almost always the better approach for preserving referential integrity and audit history while fulfilling the erasure intent. It is also reversible if the legal basis for processing is re-established, unlike deletion."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_11", number: 11, question: "When designing a GDPR-compliant consent management model in Salesforce, how should consent preferences be stored and linked to contact records to ensure they are propagated to all downstream systems?", answer: "Model: create a Consent__c object (or use the native Individual object with Data Privacy records) with fields: ConsentType__c (email marketing, data processing, etc.), ConsentStatus__c (Granted, Withdrawn), ConsentDate__c, ConsentSource__c, and a lookup to Contact/Lead. Store one record per consent type per contact. Link to Contact via lookup. Propagation: when consent changes in Salesforce, publish a Platform Event or CDC event; downstream systems (Marketing Cloud, external CRM) subscribe and update their local consent flags. Use a consent timestamp to handle ordering (newer consent always wins). For Marketing Cloud, use the standard Marketing Cloud Connector and the Individual/ContactPointConsent objects if available. Audit all consent changes via Field History Tracking or Shield Field Audit Trail."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s14_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_an_a_12", number: 12, question: "How should data residency requirements (e.g., EU data must not leave EU jurisdiction) influence Salesforce org topology decisions, and what Salesforce features support geographic data residency?", answer: "Salesforce offers data residency options via: (1) Hyperforce: Salesforce's public cloud infrastructure allows customers to choose which hyperscaler region (AWS EU, Azure EU) their org's data resides in. EU orgs on Hyperforce can be hosted entirely within EU jurisdiction. (2) Local Data Residency product: available in select regions (Germany, France, Australia) to ensure data storage within national borders. Topology implications: if EU data cannot leave the EU, a separate EU Salesforce org must be provisioned (not consolidated into a US org). This creates a multi-org architecture requiring federation rather than consolidation. Architects must map which data elements are covered by residency requirements and ensure those fields/objects are in the EU-hosted org. This directly conflicts with single-org consolidation strategies.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 15: Compare and contrast various approaches and considerations f (8 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches_",
            title: "Compare and contrast various approaches and considerations for designing and implementing an enterprise data governance program",
            color: Color(red: 0.25, green: 0.55, blue: 0.70),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.95, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "globe",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__obj",
                    title: "Compare and contrast various approaches and considerations for designing and implementing an enterprise data governance program",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__1", number: 1, question: "What are the key components of an enterprise data governance program (data stewardship, data quality metrics, metadata management, data catalog)?", answer: "Data Stewardship: assigning ownership of data domains to business users (stewards) who are accountable for data quality and definition in their domain. Data Quality Metrics: quantitative measures of data health (completeness %, accuracy rate, duplicate rate, timeliness). Tracked in dashboards and reports; threshold violations trigger remediation. Metadata Management: governing the definition, lineage, and usage of data elements, including business glossary, technical metadata (field definitions, data types), and operational metadata (when data was last updated). Data Catalog: a searchable inventory of all data assets (objects, fields, datasets, reports), their owners, classifications, and lineage. In Salesforce, a data catalog is often built with third-party tools (Collibra, Alation) or custom solutions using the Tooling API to extract metadata."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__2", number: 2, question: "How does a data stewardship model (assigning business owners to data domains) integrate with Salesforce org governance to maintain data quality over time?", answer: "Data stewards are assigned ownership of specific Salesforce objects or data domains. Their responsibilities: (1) Approve new field/object additions (prevent schema sprawl). (2) Monitor data quality dashboards for their domain (duplicate rates, null %, format errors). (3) Review and approve picklist value changes (maintain controlled vocabularies). (4) Champion data entry standards with their business teams. Integration with Salesforce: create a custom DataSteward__c mapping (or use Custom Metadata) linking each object's API name to a steward User record. Build dashboards with data quality metrics per object, assigned to the steward's home page. Use Change Management processes (Change Set review, sandbox testing policy) that require steward approval for schema changes in their domain."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__3", number: 3, question: "What are the trade-offs between a centralized data governance model (single governance board) versus a federated model (domain-level stewards) when implementing Salesforce across multiple business units?", answer: "Centralized: one governance board owns all data standards, definitions, and quality rules across the entire Salesforce org. Pros: consistency, no conflicting definitions, unified reporting. Cons: bottleneck for decisions, may not understand domain-specific nuances, slower to respond to business unit needs. Federated: each business unit has domain stewards who govern their own data, with a coordinating council for shared standards. Pros: domain expertise, faster decisions for BU-specific data, greater business buy-in. Cons: risk of divergent definitions for shared concepts (e.g., \"Customer\" means different things to Sales vs. Service), inconsistent data quality standards across BUs. Best practice: federated model with a central Data Governance Council that owns shared objects (Account, Contact) and arbitrates cross-domain conflicts."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__4", number: 4, question: "How should an enterprise data governance program define and enforce data quality thresholds for Salesforce records, and what platform features support automated data quality measurement?", answer: "Define thresholds per field and object (e.g., Account.Phone must be populated for 95% of records; Contact.Email must be valid format for 100%). Enforcement mechanisms: (1) Validation rules: enforce format and completeness at save time. (2) Required fields: enforce mandatory entry. (3) Scheduled Apex batch jobs: query records violating quality rules and create Tasks or Cases for stewards to remediate. (4) Salesforce Reports/Dashboards: build data quality dashboards showing null rates, format error rates, and duplicate counts per object. (5) Einstein Data Detect (part of Shield): automatically identifies PII fields that may be unclassified. (6) Duplicate Jobs: periodic execution to surface duplicates. Define SLA-based remediation workflows so that quality violations are resolved within a defined timeframe."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__5", number: 5, question: "What role does metadata governance play in managing Salesforce org evolution, and how should an organization track changes to object schemas, field definitions, and automation as part of its governance program?", answer: "Metadata governance ensures that changes to the Salesforce schema (new fields, modified picklists, new objects, automation rules) are tracked, reviewed, and approved before deployment. Mechanisms: (1) Version control (Git) for all metadata—every change is a commit with author, date, and description. (2) Change request process: new fields require business justification, naming standard compliance, and data steward approval before development. (3) Metadata comparison tools (Copado, Gearset) diff production vs. sandbox to identify undocumented changes. (4) Field usage analysis: periodically check which fields are actually populated (via SOQL) to identify unused fields for deprecation. (5) Object and field description fields in Salesforce: mandate that all custom fields have a Description and are linked to the business data dictionary. This prevents org technical debt accumulation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__6", number: 6, question: "How does Salesforce Shield Event Monitoring support a data governance program by providing visibility into who accessed what data and when, and what are the limitations of Event Monitoring compared to a full SIEM integration?", answer: "Shield Event Monitoring logs every significant user action: logins, report exports, record views, API queries, field access (with Field-Level Security). Logs are stored as EventLogFile objects, queryable via SOQL or downloadable as CSV. Use cases: detect data exfiltration (bulk record exports), unauthorized access to sensitive records, compliance auditing (\"who accessed Patient X's record?\"). Limitations vs. SIEM: (1) Event logs are available hourly or daily—not truly real-time alerting. (2) Storage retention is limited (up to 30 days without Real-Time Event Monitoring). (3) Salesforce Event Monitoring does not correlate events with events from non-Salesforce systems. A full SIEM (Splunk, IBM QRadar) ingests Event Monitoring logs alongside network, AD, and other system logs for holistic, real-time threat detection and cross-system correlation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__7", number: 7, question: "How should a data retention schedule be enforced in Salesforce using automation (flows, Apex batch, Privacy Center retention policies) while preserving records that are subject to a legal hold?", answer: "Retention enforcement: (1) Privacy Center Retention Policies: configure object-level retention periods; Privacy Center automatically deletes or anonymizes records past the retention date. (2) Scheduled Apex Batch: query records by creation/close date exceeding the retention period and delete or archive them. (3) Flows (Scheduled Flow): trigger deletion/archive logic on a schedule for lower-volume objects. Legal hold handling: implement a LegalHold__c boolean field on relevant objects. In all retention automation logic, add a filter: WHERE LegalHold__c = false. Records flagged for legal hold are exempt from automated deletion regardless of age. Legal hold flags should only be managed by authorized users (Legal team) via a dedicated Permission Set. Document the hold process and monitor hold records with an expiry date (HoldExpiry__c) to release them when litigation concludes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s15_compare_and_contrast_various_approaches__8", number: 8, question: "What are the governance considerations specific to California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) compliance in Salesforce that differ from GDPR, particularly around opt-out of sale and data inventory?", answer: "CCPA vs. GDPR differences in Salesforce: (1) Opt-out of sale: CCPA requires businesses to honor \"Do Not Sell My Personal Information\" requests. Model this as a dedicated opt-out flag (DoNotSell__c) on Contact, linked to a Consent record. This flag must be propagated to all downstream systems (ad networks, data brokers). GDPR has no equivalent \"sale\" concept. (2) Data inventory: CCPA requires a comprehensive inventory of what personal data is collected, why, and with whom it is shared. Use Salesforce data classification metadata and Privacy Center data maps for this, but CCPA's scope includes data shared with third parties, requiring integration with a broader data catalog. (3) CCPA does not require explicit consent for processing (unlike GDPR). Opt-out is the model, not opt-in. (4) CCPA applies to California residents; geography-based filtering in Salesforce (State/Country = California) must drive which contacts receive CCPA-specific consent management workflows.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 16: Given a customer scenario, design a data model that scales c (13 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data_",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, design a data model that scales considering LDV and solution performance",
            color: Color(red: 0.70, green: 0.30, blue: 0.40),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.90, blue: 0.92),
            icon: "person.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, design a data model that scales considering LDV and solution performance",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__1", number: 1, question: "What are the SOQL query performance implications of having 100 million records in a custom object, and how should the data model be designed to leverage standard indexes effectively?", answer: "With 100M records, non-selective queries result in full table scans that time out or return errors. Standard indexed fields (Id, Name, OwnerId, CreatedDate, SystemModStamp, RecordTypeId, Master-Detail parent ID, External ID fields, and custom indexed fields) enable the query optimizer to use an index scan rather than a full table scan. Data model design: (1) Filter queries using indexed fields in WHERE clauses. (2) Avoid querying on formula fields, long text areas, or non-indexed custom fields. (3) For date-range queries, always include a selective filter on an indexed field alongside the date range. (4) Design the data model so that common access patterns (e.g., \"records for a specific account\") use a lookup to an indexed field (AccountId is indexed by default)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__2", number: 2, question: "What is index selectivity in Salesforce, and how do the 10% and 30% thresholds for selective and semi-selective indexes influence query plan decisions?", answer: "Index selectivity measures what percentage of records in a table a SOQL WHERE clause filter will return. Selective: filter returns less than 10% of total records (or less than 333,333 records, whichever is lower for very large objects). The query optimizer uses the index—fast. Semi-selective: filter returns 10-30% of records. The optimizer may or may not use the index depending on cost estimation. Non-selective: filter returns more than 30% of records. The optimizer performs a full table scan. In practice: design data models so that common query filters are on fields where the filter criteria will be selective (e.g., Status = \"Active\" on an object where 90% of records are Inactive is selective; Status = \"Active\" where 95% are Active is non-selective). Null values do not count toward selectivity because they are not indexed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__3", number: 3, question: "When is a custom index appropriate for a Salesforce field versus relying on the standard indexed fields, and what selectivity thresholds determine whether a custom index will be used by the query optimizer?", answer: "Request a custom index from Salesforce Support when: a non-indexed field is frequently used in WHERE clauses on an LDV object, and the field values are selective (filter returns less than 10% of records). Custom indexes are appropriate for: frequently queried picklist fields (e.g., Region__c, Status__c if selective), frequently queried text fields used as identifiers, and date fields used as date range anchors when combined with another selective filter. Custom indexes are NOT useful for: fields used in non-selective filters (more than 30% of records), formula fields (cannot be indexed), long text area fields, and fields used only in ORDER BY (indexes help filtering more than sorting in Salesforce). The same 10% (selective) and 30% (semi-selective) thresholds apply to custom indexes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__4", number: 4, question: "How does adding a WHERE clause filter on a non-selective field in a SOQL query on an LDV object cause full-table scans, and what design changes prevent this?", answer: "When a WHERE clause filter references a non-indexed field or a field whose values are non-selective (more than 30% of records match), the query optimizer cannot use an index to narrow the result set. It must scan every row in the table—a full table scan. On an LDV object with 100M+ records, this takes too long and results in a query timeout or a SOQL QueryException. Design changes to prevent full-table scans: (1) Add a highly selective indexed field to the WHERE clause (e.g., combine date range with AccountId = :specificId). (2) Request a custom index on the non-indexed field if it will be selective. (3) Replace the non-selective filter with a more selective one. (4) For large analytical workloads, use Bulk API 2.0 with partitioned extracts or route queries through an external analytics tool such as CRM Analytics, which operates against a replicated data store rather than live org tables. (5) Add a skinny table containing the specific field combination frequently queried together."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__5", number: 5, question: "Why are null values not indexed by the Salesforce query optimizer, and how should a data model or query be designed to avoid unintended full-table scans caused by null-heavy fields used in filters?", answer: "Salesforce does not index null values because indexes are designed to speed up equality and range comparisons—null (absence of value) does not fit cleanly into a B-tree index structure. Consequently, a query with WHERE MyField__c = null always performs a full table scan, regardless of whether MyField__c has a custom index. Similarly, if 80% of records have a null value for an indexed field and you filter for non-null values, only 20% of records are indexed—the 80% nulls make the index appear less useful in cost estimation. Design mitigations: (1) Replace null with a sentinel default value (e.g., \"N/A\") that can be indexed. (2) Avoid using nullable fields as primary WHERE clause filters on LDV objects. (3) Add a non-null indexed field as a co-filter. (4) If nulls are unavoidable, use index-driven SOQL with a composite selective filter or route the analytical workload to Bulk API 2.0 with partitioned date-range extraction."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__6", number: 6, question: "How does the Salesforce Query Plan tool help diagnose slow SOQL queries on LDV objects, and what output from the tool indicates that a full table scan is occurring?", answer: "The Query Plan tool (available in Developer Console > Help > Preferences > Enable Query Plan) analyzes a SOQL query and shows the optimizer's execution strategy without running the query. It outputs: the operation type (Index, TableScan, Custom Index), the leading operation cost (lower is better), and the fields/indexes being used. A full table scan is indicated by: (1) Operation type \"TableScan\" appearing in the plan. (2) A high cardinality value in the plan details. (3) Cost exceeding 1.0 (the optimizer considers a table scan cheaper than index use when selectivity is poor). To remediate: modify the WHERE clause to add selective indexed filters, request a custom index, or restructure the query."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__7", number: 7, question: "How does data archiving to a Big Object reduce LDV query performance problems on the active custom object, and what are the trade-offs of this approach?", answer: "Archiving inactive records to a Big Object removes them from the active custom object's table, reducing the total row count. Fewer rows means index scans cover less data, query selectivity thresholds are easier to meet, and report timeouts are less frequent. The trade-off: archived records in a Big Object can only be queried with index-based queries matching the Big Object's composite index prefix—no ad hoc SOQL, no standard reports, and no automation. Access to archived data requires custom Apex or Lightning components. This is acceptable when archived data is accessed rarely (e.g., compliance lookups) but not when users need regular access to historical records."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__8", number: 8, question: "An LDV custom object stores 200 million activity records, 95% of which are in a status of \"Inactive\". How should the data model and query strategy be designed to serve the 5% of active records efficiently?", answer: "Design: (1) Add a custom index (requested via Salesforce Support) on Status__c. Because only 5% of records are \"Active\", the index is highly selective. (2) Structure all operational queries to include Status__c = \"Active\" as the leading WHERE clause filter, leveraging the index. (3) Store inactive records in a separate Archive__c Big Object or external data lake to reduce the active object's row count and improve all query performance. (4) Add a skinny table containing the most frequently queried fields of active records (requested via Salesforce Support). (5) For inactive record access, use index-driven SOQL with partitioned date-range filters or Bulk API 2.0 batch extraction rather than synchronous SOQL—avoiding governor limit concerns for large result sets."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__9", number: 9, question: "What are the LDV-specific implications of using standard Salesforce reports and list views on an object with tens of millions of records, and how should an architect mitigate report timeouts?", answer: "Standard reports and list views have a 10-minute timeout for query execution. On LDV objects with tens of millions of records, reports with non-selective filters or complex cross-object joins routinely timeout. List views may also timeout or return partial results. Mitigations: (1) Require all reports on LDV objects to use indexed fields in filter criteria to avoid table scans. (2) Architect pre-aggregated summary records (daily/weekly rollups stored in a summary object) that reports query instead of the LDV object directly. (3) Use external analytics (CRM Analytics or Tableau) that query data from a replicated data store rather than live Salesforce data—this is the most scalable approach for analytical workloads. (4) Use Bulk API 2.0 with partitioned date-range extraction to pull large datasets into a data warehouse for offline reporting. (5) Train report builders on filter selectivity requirements for LDV objects."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__10", number: 10, question: "How does data skew interact with LDV to compound sharing calculation performance issues, and what combined architectural approaches address both problems simultaneously?", answer: "LDV alone stresses query performance. Skew alone stresses sharing recalculation. Together: in an LDV object with skewed ownership (one user owns millions of records), sharing recalculation must process millions of sharing rows for one owner, while the sharing row table itself has billions of rows—compounding both query and write performance degradation. Combined approaches: (1) Raise OWD to Public Read Only or Public Read/Write—eliminates sharing rows entirely, solving both skew and LDV sharing problems simultaneously. (2) Partition records across multiple owners using queues. (3) Archive historical records to Big Objects (removes them from sharing calculation scope). (4) Use Role Hierarchy access instead of explicit sharing rules (fewer sharing rows). Address both root causes together rather than separately, as solving only one leaves the other unresolved."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__11", number: 11, question: "What was Async SOQL, and given its retirement by Salesforce, what modern alternatives should architects use for LDV analytics workloads?", answer: "Async SOQL was a Salesforce feature that allowed SOQL queries to execute asynchronously outside of governor limits, with results stored in a target Big Object or CSV export. It has been fully retired by Salesforce and is no longer available in any Salesforce org. Modern architects must rely on the following alternatives for large-scale analytics and LDV workloads: (1) Standard index-driven SOQL with selective filters—design queries to use indexed fields so the optimizer avoids full table scans. (2) Bulk API 2.0 with partitioned date-range or ID-range extraction—pull large datasets in parallel batches to an external system for processing. (3) CRM Analytics (Einstein Analytics)—connects to Salesforce data via a separate analytics engine, supports large-scale aggregation and reporting without impacting live org performance. (4) External data lakes (AWS S3, Snowflake, Azure Data Lake) populated via Salesforce Data Export or Bulk API, queried with purpose-built analytics tools. (5) Scheduled Apex Batch jobs—aggregate and summarize LDV data into lightweight summary records that can be queried with standard SOQL."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__12", number: 12, question: "What is a skinny table in Salesforce, when should it be requested, and what are its limitations in terms of field types, relationship fields, and the conditions under which it improves query performance?", answer: "A skinny table is a Salesforce-managed internal table that contains a denormalized subset of frequently queried fields from a standard or custom object, maintained in sync with the main table. It improves query performance by reducing I/O—the database reads fewer columns. Request a skinny table via Salesforce Support when: an LDV object has queries consistently using the same subset of fields, and those queries are causing performance issues even with proper indexing. Limitations: (1) Can contain up to 100 fields. (2) Cannot include relationship fields (lookups to other objects) or long text areas. (3) Skinny tables are only used when the SOQL query's WHERE clause is selective (index is used). (4) Not available for all objects. (5) Salesforce manages synchronization; if the object schema changes significantly, the skinny table must be updated via Support. It is a last-resort optimization after indexing strategies are exhausted."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s16_given_a_customer_scenario_design_a_data__13", number: 13, question: "How does the division of Salesforce storage into data storage and file storage affect architectural decisions when designing an LDV data model that includes attachments or documents?", answer: "Salesforce has two storage pools: Data Storage (for records—Accounts, Contacts, custom objects, etc.) priced per user, and File Storage (for Attachments, ContentDocument/Files, documents) priced separately. LDV data models that include attachments (e.g., case attachments, contract documents) can rapidly exhaust file storage, which is more expensive than data storage per GB at scale. Architectural decisions: (1) Use Salesforce Files (ContentDocument/ContentVersion) instead of legacy Attachments—Files support external storage via Salesforce Files Connect (AWS S3, SharePoint) to store file content externally while metadata stays in Salesforce (reducing file storage consumption). (2) Set file size limits via org-wide settings. (3) Archive old files to external storage after a defined age. (4) Monitor storage consumption via Setup > Company Information and build alerts when approaching limits.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 17: Given a customer scenario, recommend a data archiving and pu (5 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s17_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_da",
            title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend a data archiving and purging plan that is optimal for the customer's data storage management needs",
            color: Color(red: 0.40, green: 0.60, blue: 0.30),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.96, blue: 0.90),
            icon: "leaf.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s17_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_da_obj",
                    title: "Given a customer scenario, recommend a data archiving and purging plan that is optimal for the customer's data storage management needs",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_da_1", number: 1, question: "How should a data retention policy be implemented in Salesforce to automatically purge records that exceed a defined age, and what automation tools are available for this?", answer: "Implementation options: (1) Privacy Center Retention Policies: configure object-level retention periods; Privacy Center automatically identifies and deletes (or anonymizes) records past retention age. Best for GDPR-aligned workflows. (2) Scheduled Apex Batch: query records where CreatedDate < :retentionCutoff AND LegalHold__c = false, then delete in batches of 200. Handles complex logic and multi-object cascades. (3) Scheduled Flow (Record-Triggered or Scheduled): for simpler, lower-volume objects, a scheduled flow can delete qualifying records. (4) Bulk API with external orchestration: extract IDs of expired records via SOQL, delete via Bulk API from an external job. Regardless of tool, always implement a LegalHold bypass, log purge actions for audit, and test in sandbox with production-scale data volumes before enabling in production."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_da_2", number: 2, question: "What are the data recovery and accessibility trade-offs of soft-delete archiving (moving to a hidden archive object) versus hard-delete purging in Salesforce, and when should each be preferred?", answer: "Soft-delete archiving: records are moved to a hidden archive object (or Big Object) rather than deleted. The data remains accessible via query (with different access patterns) and can be restored. Preferred when: records may need to be retrieved for legal discovery, regulatory audit, or business re-processing; and when \"deletion\" would break referential integrity with related records. Hard-delete purging: records are permanently deleted (bypassing the Recycle Bin using Database.delete with allOrNone and emptyRecycleBin call). Storage is freed immediately. Cannot be recovered. Preferred when: data has zero future value, storage cost is the primary driver, and regulatory requirements confirm permanent deletion is acceptable. Always prefer soft-delete archiving for records with any potential future regulatory, legal, or business need."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_da_3", number: 3, question: "What are the trade-offs between archiving Salesforce data to Big Objects versus exporting to an external data lake (e.g., AWS S3) in terms of accessibility, cost, and compliance?", answer: "Big Objects: archived data stays within the Salesforce ecosystem, is queryable via index-based SOQL using the composite index prefix (not standard ad hoc SOQL), can be displayed in the Salesforce UI via custom components, and benefits from Salesforce's security model. Storage is very large relative to standard objects but is not free. External data lake (S3): much cheaper per GB, supports powerful external analytics (Athena, Spark, Snowflake), but data exits the Salesforce security boundary (requires separate access controls). Not natively queryable from Salesforce; requires integration to surface in the UI. Compliance: for data that must remain within a specific jurisdiction or under Salesforce's security controls, Big Objects are preferable. For cost-efficient, analytics-heavy long-term archiving where data accessibility outside Salesforce is acceptable, external data lake is preferred."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_da_4", number: 4, question: "A customer needs to retain 7 years of closed case records for legal compliance but wants to reduce active org storage. What archiving strategy best balances retention, accessibility, and cost?", answer: "Recommended strategy: (1) Archive closed cases older than 12 months to a Big Object (ClosedCaseArchive__b) containing the key case fields needed for legal review. Preserve the Case ID and all identifying/audit fields. (2) Alternatively, export closed cases to an external data lake (S3/Snowflake) for cheaper long-term storage and richer analytics. (3) If agents occasionally need to view archived cases: build a custom Lightning component that queries the Big Object using its composite index or calls the external system API to display archived case data on the Account page. (4) Hard-delete the original Salesforce Case records after successful archive verification to free data storage. (5) Implement a Legal Hold flag to exempt specific cases from archiving during active litigation. This approach meets the 7-year retention requirement while reducing active org storage costs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s17_given_a_customer_scenario_recommend_a_da_5", number: 5, question: "How do Salesforce data storage limits (file storage versus data storage) and their associated costs influence the decision to archive versus purge, and what options exist for reducing file storage costs in particular?", answer: "Data storage (records) and file storage (Attachments, Files/ContentDocument) are separately metered. File storage is typically more expensive per GB and fills faster in orgs with heavy document attachment usage (e.g., contract management, case attachments). Archiving records reduces data storage; archiving files requires a different approach. Options for file storage reduction: (1) Salesforce Files Connect with external storage (AWS S3, SharePoint via MuleSoft or native connector): stores file content externally while metadata and links remain in Salesforce. Reduces file storage consumption significantly. (2) Hard-delete old Attachments and ContentDocuments past a defined age (and emptyRecycleBin to free storage immediately). (3) Compress or deduplicate files at the integration layer before uploading. The decision between archive (retaining data at lower cost) vs. purge (freeing storage entirely) must align with legal hold requirements and the organization's data retention policy.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

